WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.138 --> 00:00:04.078
I mean, if we wanna turn the death of the earth into capital,

00:00:04.078 --> 00:00:05.219
we're doing a good job.

00:00:05.219 --> 00:00:07.235
And then what do you do with that capital?

00:00:07.235 --> 00:00:09.353
I mean, what meaning does money have

00:00:09.353 --> 00:00:11.903
if it's an unlivable planet.

00:00:11.903 --> 00:00:14.486
(upbeat music)

00:00:30.796 --> 00:00:33.127
Greetings earth dwellers, welcome to the podcast.

00:00:33.127 --> 00:00:33.986
My guest today,

00:00:33.986 --> 00:00:35.835
back for his second appearance on the show

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:38.002
is an icon, a true legend.

00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:41.888
One of the environmental movements leading voices,

00:00:41.888 --> 00:00:43.267
Paul Hawken.

00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:45.427
In addition to his profound work

00:00:45.427 --> 00:00:48.178
as a planetary change agent,

00:00:48.178 --> 00:00:50.207
Paul is also an entrepreneur.

00:00:50.207 --> 00:00:54.006
He founded both Erewhon markets and Smith and Hawken

00:00:54.006 --> 00:00:55.657
and author of eight books,

00:00:55.657 --> 00:00:56.827
including the groundbreaking

00:00:56.827 --> 00:00:59.477
New York times bestseller Drawdowm.

00:00:59.477 --> 00:01:03.215
Now Paul is back with an astonishing, beautiful new book

00:01:03.215 --> 00:01:04.925
called Regeneration,

00:01:04.925 --> 00:01:08.727
ending the climate crisis in one generation,

00:01:08.727 --> 00:01:11.807
as well as a corresponding nonprofit by the same name,

00:01:11.807 --> 00:01:13.183
regeneration.org,

00:01:13.183 --> 00:01:17.397
a collective work that together aims to guide and inspire

00:01:17.397 --> 00:01:19.899
the burgeoning climate movement.

00:01:19.899 --> 00:01:23.797
Paul is a friend, he's a mentor, he's a lighthouse

00:01:23.797 --> 00:01:27.065
and a man who has indelibly shaped my perspective,

00:01:27.065 --> 00:01:29.186
my philosophy and my actions

00:01:29.186 --> 00:01:32.168
when it comes to ecological responsibility.

00:01:32.168 --> 00:01:34.266
It's an honor to host him today.

00:01:34.266 --> 00:01:37.037
This is a remarkable exchange

00:01:37.037 --> 00:01:38.420
and my intention and hope

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.677
is that this message will do for you

00:01:40.677 --> 00:01:43.936
what it has and continues to do for me.

00:01:43.936 --> 00:01:46.519
So please click that subscribe button

00:01:46.519 --> 00:01:49.880
and notification bell and enjoy my conversation

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.576
with the great Paul Hawken.

00:01:52.576 --> 00:01:54.027
Well, let's talk about the environment.

00:01:54.027 --> 00:01:55.120
Okay. Let's talk about the environment.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.036
Should we do that?
Yeah.

00:01:56.036 --> 00:01:57.119
It's good to see you first of all.

00:01:57.119 --> 00:01:57.952
Really good to see you.

00:01:57.952 --> 00:01:59.113
Yeah, thank you for coming.

00:01:59.113 --> 00:02:00.544
I hear you so much you know,

00:02:00.544 --> 00:02:02.625
like I feel like I'm in your presence.

00:02:02.625 --> 00:02:04.653
I appreciate that.

00:02:04.653 --> 00:02:06.212
No, I am.

00:02:06.212 --> 00:02:09.123
Yeah, we had all these plans of getting together

00:02:09.123 --> 00:02:10.182
and doing all kinds of stuff

00:02:10.182 --> 00:02:13.682
and obviously the pandemic put that aside,

00:02:14.843 --> 00:02:17.055
but I'm happy to be in your presence today.

00:02:17.055 --> 00:02:19.573
Thank you so much, really.

00:02:19.573 --> 00:02:21.600
The occasion of course, being this beautiful,

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.200
extraordinary new book,

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.034
that's about to come out,

00:02:24.034 --> 00:02:27.419
regeneration ending the climate crisis in one generation,

00:02:27.419 --> 00:02:31.743
you gave me this gorgeous folio version of the book.

00:02:31.743 --> 00:02:33.951
Only a few exist in the world.

00:02:33.951 --> 00:02:34.784
Yes,

00:02:34.784 --> 00:02:35.617
Is that true.
That's true.

00:02:35.617 --> 00:02:37.448
I'm feeling very grateful for that.

00:02:37.448 --> 00:02:38.281
So thanks.

00:02:38.281 --> 00:02:41.337
Yeah, It just is a special people.

00:02:41.337 --> 00:02:42.170
It's exciting.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:45.008
It's been a minute since you've had a book out.

00:02:45.008 --> 00:02:48.184
Yeah, April 17 was the last book Drawdown

00:02:48.184 --> 00:02:50.351
and this one is coming out

00:02:51.539 --> 00:02:54.345
in about six countries almost at the same time,

00:02:54.345 --> 00:02:57.115
actually 54 countries right now in September.

00:02:57.115 --> 00:02:59.556
Right from the get go out of the gate that's unusual.

00:02:59.556 --> 00:03:01.005
That's too confident.

00:03:01.005 --> 00:03:04.025
Yeah. This time the publishers are.

00:03:04.025 --> 00:03:05.512
last time, they were kind of waiting

00:03:05.512 --> 00:03:06.993
and watching and looking.

00:03:06.993 --> 00:03:09.155
Now Drawdown is in 17 languages.

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:09.988
Right.

00:03:09.988 --> 00:03:11.394
Well, that book was a ground breaker

00:03:11.394 --> 00:03:13.754
for myself and so many people.

00:03:13.754 --> 00:03:17.087
And I feel like this book is the perfect

00:03:18.449 --> 00:03:20.154
follow up to that in that

00:03:20.154 --> 00:03:24.276
it strikes perhaps an even more optimistic tone

00:03:24.276 --> 00:03:28.351
when it comes to solving this great existential crisis

00:03:28.351 --> 00:03:29.904
that we find ourselves in.

00:03:29.904 --> 00:03:31.985
But I think the overarching theme of it

00:03:31.985 --> 00:03:34.954
is inclusion like inclusivity.

00:03:34.954 --> 00:03:37.035
And it's very empowering in the sense that

00:03:37.035 --> 00:03:40.592
it's like letting us into all these amazing things

00:03:40.592 --> 00:03:43.543
that are occurring and how we can participate in them

00:03:43.543 --> 00:03:45.843
in so many different ways.

00:03:45.843 --> 00:03:48.596
Absolutely. I'm just like everybody else in a sense.

00:03:48.596 --> 00:03:49.429
I live in a world.

00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:51.394
I look at the world and try to figure it out.

00:03:51.394 --> 00:03:55.266
I mean, I'm no different whether I'm a journalist or not,

00:03:55.266 --> 00:03:56.453
doesn't make any difference.

00:03:56.453 --> 00:03:58.293
Everyone does that to a certain extent.

00:03:58.293 --> 00:04:00.453
And a journalist might do a bit more

00:04:00.453 --> 00:04:04.255
because they try to share what they see

00:04:04.255 --> 00:04:05.683
and what I have seen

00:04:05.683 --> 00:04:09.016
and not just since Drawdown was created,

00:04:10.172 --> 00:04:11.122
but before that,

00:04:11.122 --> 00:04:13.075
but certainly since then

00:04:13.075 --> 00:04:16.585
is a world is going into crisis around climate

00:04:16.585 --> 00:04:19.502
and more rapidly than was expected.

00:04:22.796 --> 00:04:23.835
And at the same time,

00:04:23.835 --> 00:04:27.883
even though I would say a majority of the people

00:04:27.883 --> 00:04:32.644
in the United States certainly and in Europe are empathetic,

00:04:32.644 --> 00:04:37.282
sympathetic, understand the basic cause and effects.

00:04:37.282 --> 00:04:39.452
They're not doing anything.

00:04:39.452 --> 00:04:40.614
They're not engaged.

00:04:40.614 --> 00:04:42.956
So how could it be,

00:04:42.956 --> 00:04:46.701
that you see the writing on the wall,

00:04:46.701 --> 00:04:48.544
you see this impending crisis,

00:04:48.544 --> 00:04:52.989
you're getting a very visceral sense of what's happening,

00:04:52.989 --> 00:04:56.792
on the ground and floods and cold and heat

00:04:56.792 --> 00:04:58.959
and drought and et cetera.

00:04:59.939 --> 00:05:03.021
And yet there's still this disengagement.

00:05:03.021 --> 00:05:06.512
And so regeneration was really about looking at that,

00:05:06.512 --> 00:05:09.201
not blaming people for being disengaged,

00:05:09.201 --> 00:05:13.043
but trying to understand why we are not.

00:05:13.043 --> 00:05:16.343
And I'll just clear myself generally,

00:05:16.343 --> 00:05:18.426
but what would engage us?

00:05:20.009 --> 00:05:22.217
And what's prohibiting it,

00:05:22.217 --> 00:05:26.384
what's stopping us and what would open up humanity

00:05:27.806 --> 00:05:30.186
to the idea of working together?

00:05:30.186 --> 00:05:33.214
Yeah. I think from a psychological perspective,

00:05:33.214 --> 00:05:35.507
there doesn't seem to be a meaningful

00:05:35.507 --> 00:05:37.916
on-ramp for people in the sense that

00:05:37.916 --> 00:05:39.583
I think you're correct.

00:05:39.583 --> 00:05:42.275
There's an overwhelming awareness now

00:05:42.275 --> 00:05:45.195
that didn't exist perhaps even a decade ago.

00:05:45.195 --> 00:05:48.945
And I think most people are good faith actors

00:05:50.342 --> 00:05:52.184
and wanna do the right thing

00:05:52.184 --> 00:05:54.605
and recycle or do what they can,

00:05:54.605 --> 00:05:57.491
but it doesn't feel like any of these things

00:05:57.491 --> 00:05:59.304
amount to very much, right?

00:05:59.304 --> 00:06:01.051
Like I can take personal responsibility

00:06:01.051 --> 00:06:04.253
for a certain subset of decisions that I make every day,

00:06:04.253 --> 00:06:05.835
but is this really moving the needle?

00:06:05.835 --> 00:06:09.881
So there's a lack of agency or connectivity

00:06:09.881 --> 00:06:12.865
to the solutions that would really get people

00:06:12.865 --> 00:06:14.302
emotionally engaged

00:06:14.302 --> 00:06:18.063
and feeling like they actually are making a difference.

00:06:18.063 --> 00:06:20.230
Absolutely. I think that

00:06:22.164 --> 00:06:24.341
there's so many things you covered right there.

00:06:24.341 --> 00:06:26.841
So break it open a little bit.

00:06:27.953 --> 00:06:29.574
It's got a connectivity,

00:06:29.574 --> 00:06:33.407
which is that the root cause of global warming

00:06:34.743 --> 00:06:39.074
is a massive disconnection between each other,

00:06:39.074 --> 00:06:43.344
between people, between people in nature and nature itself,

00:06:43.344 --> 00:06:45.511
which is caused by humans,

00:06:46.443 --> 00:06:48.414
habitat fragmentation, pollution

00:06:48.414 --> 00:06:51.914
desertification of the oceans and onwards.

00:06:52.903 --> 00:06:57.236
And so regenerating and reversing the climate crisis

00:07:00.576 --> 00:07:04.743
is really about reconnecting those broken strands.

00:07:05.911 --> 00:07:09.574
And that's what the solutions really are,

00:07:09.574 --> 00:07:13.994
as opposed to sort of standalone techniques or technologies,

00:07:13.994 --> 00:07:15.744
that is going to fix it.

00:07:15.744 --> 00:07:18.713
That word fix doesn't even belong in the conversation

00:07:18.713 --> 00:07:19.773
because it's not an it,

00:07:19.773 --> 00:07:21.404
and you can't fix it.

00:07:21.404 --> 00:07:23.674
The atmosphere is the biosphere

00:07:23.674 --> 00:07:27.507
that's the same thing and we are a part of it.

00:07:29.181 --> 00:07:31.624
And nature never makes a mistake only we do.

00:07:31.624 --> 00:07:33.572
So let's look at what we're doing.

00:07:33.572 --> 00:07:36.872
And regeneration is very much about alignment

00:07:36.872 --> 00:07:38.915
with the living world,

00:07:38.915 --> 00:07:42.555
with the way it works and always has.

00:07:42.555 --> 00:07:44.222
And so, as you said,

00:07:46.635 --> 00:07:48.545
what we've done conversationally

00:07:48.545 --> 00:07:50.412
and sort of declaratively

00:07:50.412 --> 00:07:52.304
and almost imperatively

00:07:52.304 --> 00:07:54.751
is individuate the problem,

00:07:54.751 --> 00:07:58.062
which is this is what you can do or should do.

00:07:58.062 --> 00:07:59.229
And it's true.

00:08:00.971 --> 00:08:02.004
Those are good things,

00:08:02.004 --> 00:08:05.287
recycling and use cold water in your wash machine,

00:08:05.287 --> 00:08:09.394
and try not to drive an EV if you can afford it,

00:08:09.394 --> 00:08:10.703
et cetera, et cetera.

00:08:10.703 --> 00:08:15.703
But I think people understand that even if they do them,

00:08:15.863 --> 00:08:20.522
they know it's not sufficient to the task at hand.

00:08:20.522 --> 00:08:23.044
And so actually makes people more disempowered

00:08:23.044 --> 00:08:23.877
in a funny way,

00:08:23.877 --> 00:08:25.550
even though they're actually participating

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:27.343
in the kind of token way.

00:08:27.343 --> 00:08:30.176
And then they look to governments,

00:08:31.952 --> 00:08:34.273
they look to the conference of the parties,

00:08:34.273 --> 00:08:36.091
the UN framework on climate change.

00:08:36.091 --> 00:08:38.462
They look for these annual meetings,

00:08:38.462 --> 00:08:41.901
just one in Glasgow, in November

00:08:41.901 --> 00:08:42.885
for something to happen,

00:08:42.885 --> 00:08:46.577
like hoping that politicians will get together

00:08:46.577 --> 00:08:47.715
and solve the problem.

00:08:47.715 --> 00:08:50.734
If politicians knew what to do,

00:08:50.734 --> 00:08:52.566
we wouldn't be in the situation we are today.

00:08:52.566 --> 00:08:53.886
And that's just not gonna happen.

00:08:53.886 --> 00:08:55.208
Not to say we can say it.

00:08:55.208 --> 00:08:58.457
So then you feel like there's this gulf

00:08:58.457 --> 00:09:01.790
between these huge Meta or institutions,

00:09:03.539 --> 00:09:04.906
corporations and governments,

00:09:04.906 --> 00:09:06.537
and then you as an individual.

00:09:06.537 --> 00:09:07.370
And that's, again,

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:10.216
what regeneration is trying to say, whoa, wait a minute.

00:09:10.216 --> 00:09:14.368
that's not where the solution is gonna come from.

00:09:14.368 --> 00:09:15.201
Right.

00:09:16.179 --> 00:09:19.012
Regeneration is sort of bedfellows

00:09:20.916 --> 00:09:23.598
with this idea of symbiosis.

00:09:23.598 --> 00:09:26.048
Like how can we live more symbiotically on the planet

00:09:26.048 --> 00:09:27.244
and understanding that,

00:09:27.244 --> 00:09:29.705
and really embodying that

00:09:29.705 --> 00:09:34.038
begins with really fully embracing and acknowledging

00:09:35.687 --> 00:09:38.104
that we are all micro systems

00:09:39.077 --> 00:09:40.630
that are part of a macro system.

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:42.966
And it's sort of belying the idea

00:09:42.966 --> 00:09:44.168
that nature is over there

00:09:44.168 --> 00:09:45.129
and we're over here

00:09:45.129 --> 00:09:47.590
and we venture into nature and out of nature.

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:50.579
And yet, fundamentally that's just a false,

00:09:50.579 --> 00:09:52.567
because the cells in our body

00:09:52.567 --> 00:09:53.749
and everything that we're made up of

00:09:53.749 --> 00:09:57.467
is of course part and parcel of this environment.

00:09:57.467 --> 00:09:58.878
And in terms of the solution,

00:09:58.878 --> 00:10:01.045
once you acknowledge that,

00:10:02.079 --> 00:10:03.763
I think what you begin to understand

00:10:03.763 --> 00:10:07.740
what the book kind of does a beautiful job conveying

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:10.240
is the idea that there's this,

00:10:15.181 --> 00:10:17.031
the problem is systemic,

00:10:17.031 --> 00:10:19.531
that we can't truly move into,

00:10:20.432 --> 00:10:23.323
this more symbiotic regenerative relationship

00:10:23.323 --> 00:10:27.831
with the planet until we have some corrective measures

00:10:27.831 --> 00:10:29.309
with our systems at large, right?

00:10:29.309 --> 00:10:31.883
There's this gigantic misalignment of incentives

00:10:31.883 --> 00:10:36.280
across the board, be it by dent of governmental bodies

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:39.612
or the tectonic plates of capitalism,

00:10:39.612 --> 00:10:42.481
all of these forces that the individual feels powerless

00:10:42.481 --> 00:10:45.481
to have any control over or say over

00:10:47.212 --> 00:10:49.359
that perpetuate the problem.

00:10:49.359 --> 00:10:51.928
So, short of revolution,

00:10:51.928 --> 00:10:54.690
like how are we creating better incentives

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:56.559
for the systems that are in play?

00:10:56.559 --> 00:10:59.484
Or how do we create better systems

00:10:59.484 --> 00:11:01.464
with incentives that could supplant the systems

00:11:01.464 --> 00:11:04.529
that are leading us astray?

00:11:04.529 --> 00:11:06.207
That is the question.

00:11:06.207 --> 00:11:09.457
And I think that between the individual

00:11:10.888 --> 00:11:14.830
and meta institutions and global institutions

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:17.497
is something else called agency.

00:11:18.926 --> 00:11:22.093
And agency is sort of been overlooked.

00:11:24.652 --> 00:11:27.092
There is no such thing as an individual.

00:11:27.092 --> 00:11:31.740
That's sort of a delusion we wake up with in the morning.

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:36.035
And, but functionally, there's no such thing.

00:11:36.035 --> 00:11:38.436
And every person has agency.

00:11:38.436 --> 00:11:39.576
It's their family.

00:11:39.576 --> 00:11:41.172
As their friends, it's their community,

00:11:41.172 --> 00:11:42.601
it's their neighborhood, it's their churches

00:11:42.601 --> 00:11:44.551
or synagogues or school.

00:11:44.551 --> 00:11:46.040
It's where they teach, it's their city.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:48.897
It's their company, colleagues.

00:11:48.897 --> 00:11:50.275
It goes on and on and on.

00:11:50.275 --> 00:11:54.505
And we have a network we're part of networks.

00:11:54.505 --> 00:11:57.726
And that is where we have influence

00:11:57.726 --> 00:11:59.717
and where we can make a difference.

00:11:59.717 --> 00:12:02.884
And I think a lot of people have felt,

00:12:04.907 --> 00:12:06.490
come to think that,

00:12:07.896 --> 00:12:09.269
if we get renewables right,

00:12:09.269 --> 00:12:10.577
or we do this, we do that.

00:12:10.577 --> 00:12:13.459
that again, we'll fix it,

00:12:13.459 --> 00:12:17.042
but like there's some Archimedean solution.

00:12:17.994 --> 00:12:21.262
If we pull hard enough the problem is gonna go away.

00:12:21.262 --> 00:12:22.095
Right.

00:12:22.095 --> 00:12:24.400
And rather than understanding that

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:26.004
the solution is everywhere,

00:12:26.004 --> 00:12:28.953
it's ubiquitous, it's local, regional.

00:12:28.953 --> 00:12:31.036
It's where agency exists.

00:12:32.291 --> 00:12:34.863
And that is everywhere on earth

00:12:34.863 --> 00:12:36.262
with every culture,

00:12:36.262 --> 00:12:38.929
every society and every country.

00:12:40.711 --> 00:12:43.524
And so that's the good news,

00:12:43.524 --> 00:12:47.441
which is that we can solve this, we really can.

00:12:48.889 --> 00:12:49.982
but we can't solve it,

00:12:49.982 --> 00:12:52.661
if we think someone's gonna solve it for us.

00:12:52.661 --> 00:12:57.331
Or if we come to believe that individuals are to blame

00:12:57.331 --> 00:12:59.923
and that the responsible and they'll solve it,

00:12:59.923 --> 00:13:02.062
we kinda know that one's not true.

00:13:02.062 --> 00:13:03.084
Right.

00:13:03.084 --> 00:13:04.402
I'm not saying we all have,

00:13:04.402 --> 00:13:05.383
we don't have personal responsibility.

00:13:05.383 --> 00:13:06.216
Of course we do.

00:13:06.216 --> 00:13:09.394
But that alone is going to be sufficient.

00:13:09.394 --> 00:13:12.601
And so when we look at the institutions

00:13:12.601 --> 00:13:15.823
with the perverse incentives that they have,

00:13:15.823 --> 00:13:20.823
the economic institutions, the political institutions,

00:13:20.839 --> 00:13:23.534
they are all extractive.

00:13:23.534 --> 00:13:26.733
That is to say every institution that we've come to know

00:13:26.733 --> 00:13:30.761
and trust or not trust, but buy from, or believe in,

00:13:30.761 --> 00:13:33.513
or invest in or own shares in,

00:13:33.513 --> 00:13:34.882
is extractive.

00:13:34.882 --> 00:13:37.124
And there was this taking life.

00:13:37.124 --> 00:13:39.498
And we sort of take that for granted

00:13:39.498 --> 00:13:41.415
that don't make a mess,

00:13:43.793 --> 00:13:45.101
we'll clean up your mess,

00:13:45.101 --> 00:13:46.014
that kind of thing.

00:13:46.014 --> 00:13:48.005
And that's sustainability in a way,

00:13:48.005 --> 00:13:50.385
but extraction is taking life.

00:13:50.385 --> 00:13:51.465
And when you take life,

00:13:51.465 --> 00:13:52.614
that's degeneration,

00:13:52.614 --> 00:13:55.212
that's what degeneration is when you take life.

00:13:55.212 --> 00:13:59.129
And so regeneration is really not about blaming

00:14:02.183 --> 00:14:04.016
and sort of demonizing

00:14:07.286 --> 00:14:11.614
those institutions or those economic sectors

00:14:11.614 --> 00:14:13.891
so much the same way.

00:14:13.891 --> 00:14:17.243
that road, that degeneration road doesn't go much further.

00:14:17.243 --> 00:14:19.245
That's everything's screaming at us,

00:14:19.245 --> 00:14:22.383
all the science, our experiences, and so forth.

00:14:22.383 --> 00:14:24.103
Like that road doesn't go much further.

00:14:24.103 --> 00:14:25.465
Why are we going down that road?

00:14:25.465 --> 00:14:28.234
And so regeneration is about a 180 pivot.

00:14:28.234 --> 00:14:31.063
Like, can we just stop and go the other way?

00:14:31.063 --> 00:14:35.725
Can we not regenerate the world and have a GDP

00:14:35.725 --> 00:14:39.323
and an economy and jobs rather than degenerate it?

00:14:39.323 --> 00:14:41.794
And the fact is we can,

00:14:41.794 --> 00:14:44.035
and it's really a question of healing,

00:14:44.035 --> 00:14:45.454
the future of stealing the future,

00:14:45.454 --> 00:14:47.965
because what we're doing is stealing the future.

00:14:47.965 --> 00:14:49.785
Now it used to be from our children.

00:14:49.785 --> 00:14:51.784
Now it's from ourselves practically.

00:14:51.784 --> 00:14:54.034
And so it really isn't like

00:14:57.764 --> 00:15:01.093
I could say something that what's happening

00:15:01.093 --> 00:15:02.643
with respect to climate is wrong

00:15:02.643 --> 00:15:03.733
or not at all.

00:15:03.733 --> 00:15:04.566
It's all great.

00:15:04.566 --> 00:15:06.462
What everybody's doing is fantastic.

00:15:06.462 --> 00:15:08.672
But unless we actually do address

00:15:08.672 --> 00:15:11.904
those institutional incentives,

00:15:11.904 --> 00:15:15.782
and actually assumptions that are so deep,

00:15:15.782 --> 00:15:20.222
that people don't even understand them as assumptions,

00:15:20.222 --> 00:15:23.139
then we won't have a chance at all,

00:15:24.849 --> 00:15:26.727
because the...

00:15:26.727 --> 00:15:30.810
one of the things to go back to the fix it thing,

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:33.609
like bill gates and others,

00:15:33.609 --> 00:15:35.757
like John Kerry, for example, are saying,

00:15:35.757 --> 00:15:39.338
if we don't do nuclear, we're screwed, okay.

00:15:39.338 --> 00:15:43.567
And this idea that there is this one thing,

00:15:43.567 --> 00:15:47.056
but you could have renewable energy

00:15:47.056 --> 00:15:50.838
or nuclear energy for the whole world today.

00:15:50.838 --> 00:15:52.797
And we'd still be going right over the cliff

00:15:52.797 --> 00:15:55.308
because we're destroying all the living systems on earth.

00:15:55.308 --> 00:15:57.615
We're destroying our oceans, our fisheries, our land,

00:15:57.615 --> 00:16:00.866
our forest, our insects our pollinators.

00:16:00.866 --> 00:16:02.965
And so that has nothing to do with renewable energy

00:16:02.965 --> 00:16:04.155
or fossil fuel energy,

00:16:04.155 --> 00:16:06.076
that has to do with us.

00:16:06.076 --> 00:16:08.046
And so we have to see it, as you said earlier,

00:16:08.046 --> 00:16:09.515
as the system is whole,

00:16:09.515 --> 00:16:13.634
you can't silo it and separate it out and say,

00:16:13.634 --> 00:16:15.666
we're gonna do that and do this and do that.

00:16:15.666 --> 00:16:17.297
You can do that,

00:16:17.297 --> 00:16:21.047
but unless you step back and look at it systemically,

00:16:21.047 --> 00:16:23.546
you're not gonna get to the core cause or core cure.

00:16:23.546 --> 00:16:26.335
But the idea being with this,

00:16:26.335 --> 00:16:29.242
kind of typical optimistic flare

00:16:29.242 --> 00:16:31.334
that you have being that,

00:16:31.334 --> 00:16:35.563
we can create a non extractive relationship

00:16:35.563 --> 00:16:37.876
with the planet without having to,

00:16:37.876 --> 00:16:39.962
throw capitalism to the wind,

00:16:39.962 --> 00:16:42.629
like within certain confines of the system

00:16:42.629 --> 00:16:44.087
that we've erected,

00:16:44.087 --> 00:16:49.087
we can course correct and still create proper incentives.

00:16:49.087 --> 00:16:50.971
I mean the extraction would, I guess,

00:16:50.971 --> 00:16:53.049
would be the economic benefit

00:16:53.049 --> 00:16:57.477
that someone would get out of changing the relationship

00:16:57.477 --> 00:17:01.771
with the planet to be symbiotic and regenerative.

00:17:01.771 --> 00:17:04.130
Well, the capitalist system really takes life

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:06.319
and turns it into money.

00:17:06.319 --> 00:17:07.152
Right.

00:17:07.152 --> 00:17:07.985
It squeezes every last...

00:17:07.985 --> 00:17:10.241
And being extractive by its very nature.

00:17:10.241 --> 00:17:11.074
Right.

00:17:11.074 --> 00:17:13.909
But you're saying that still within that

00:17:13.909 --> 00:17:17.537
we can move in a better direction here.

00:17:17.537 --> 00:17:18.565
What is that?

00:17:18.565 --> 00:17:19.706
That's a really good question

00:17:19.706 --> 00:17:22.899
because nobody invented the capitalist system.

00:17:22.899 --> 00:17:24.707
It was named after the fact

00:17:24.707 --> 00:17:27.540
in a sense economic systems arise.

00:17:28.579 --> 00:17:32.120
And then we kind of tag them later.

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:34.937
I don't know what a regenerative economic system

00:17:34.937 --> 00:17:35.806
will be called.

00:17:35.806 --> 00:17:38.197
I don't think it'll be called capitalistic.

00:17:38.197 --> 00:17:42.947
It doesn't mean that existing institutions and businesses

00:17:44.189 --> 00:17:46.879
have to go out of business and we need to replace them.

00:17:46.879 --> 00:17:48.298
I'm not saying that at all,

00:17:48.298 --> 00:17:50.798
but the advent of the big core

00:17:52.269 --> 00:17:54.609
and the different types of corporations

00:17:54.609 --> 00:17:58.327
and so forth that have a very different priority

00:17:58.327 --> 00:18:00.494
in terms of their purpose.

00:18:03.216 --> 00:18:04.049
Yeah.

00:18:04.049 --> 00:18:05.319
And part of that priority

00:18:05.319 --> 00:18:08.918
is short-term gains over long-term.

00:18:08.918 --> 00:18:09.751
Right.

00:18:09.751 --> 00:18:12.299
You know, well-being for all, right?

00:18:12.299 --> 00:18:13.457
Absolutely.

00:18:13.457 --> 00:18:16.691
So that's a tough one to tackle.

00:18:16.691 --> 00:18:17.524
Well, I mean,

00:18:17.524 --> 00:18:20.937
if we wanna turn the death of the earth into capital,

00:18:20.937 --> 00:18:22.045
we're doing a good job.

00:18:22.045 --> 00:18:24.170
And then what are you gonna do with that capital?

00:18:24.170 --> 00:18:26.283
I mean, what meaning does money have

00:18:26.283 --> 00:18:27.923
if it's an unlivable planet?

00:18:27.923 --> 00:18:29.050
And so I think,

00:18:29.050 --> 00:18:31.981
but I do think you're seeing in corporations

00:18:31.981 --> 00:18:33.398
a real big shift.

00:18:34.689 --> 00:18:38.092
I'm not talking about the old commitment

00:18:38.092 --> 00:18:40.792
to net zero by 2050, all that sort of stuff.

00:18:40.792 --> 00:18:42.003
Commitments don't mean a thing.

00:18:42.003 --> 00:18:42.923
Goals don't mean a thing.

00:18:42.923 --> 00:18:43.756
They don't do anything.

00:18:43.756 --> 00:18:44.589
They're not actions.

00:18:44.589 --> 00:18:46.952
They're just commitments, just words.

00:18:46.952 --> 00:18:51.384
But in my experience in the last couple of years

00:18:51.384 --> 00:18:53.233
and even during COVID,

00:18:53.233 --> 00:18:54.816
what you're seeing,

00:18:56.544 --> 00:19:00.152
when you talk to CEOs of very large companies,

00:19:00.152 --> 00:19:02.051
very large ones,

00:19:02.051 --> 00:19:03.798
the largest in the world

00:19:03.798 --> 00:19:06.131
is their eyes are different.

00:19:08.151 --> 00:19:10.083
What I mean by that is

00:19:10.083 --> 00:19:12.916
they're not being nice and saying,

00:19:14.064 --> 00:19:16.403
yo, we have to renew our social license

00:19:16.403 --> 00:19:20.091
and do more sustainability and blah, blah, blah.

00:19:20.091 --> 00:19:23.413
Which is what they've been doing, kind of,

00:19:23.413 --> 00:19:26.080
they get it, they have children,

00:19:27.923 --> 00:19:30.852
they have brothers and sisters, have family.

00:19:30.852 --> 00:19:32.283
That's a pretty big paradigm shift.

00:19:32.283 --> 00:19:33.223
It's a really big one.

00:19:33.223 --> 00:19:35.664
and they find themselves getting it

00:19:35.664 --> 00:19:36.584
so to speak,

00:19:36.584 --> 00:19:38.274
when they're at the helm

00:19:38.274 --> 00:19:40.691
of these extraordinary corporations

00:19:40.691 --> 00:19:43.074
that have hundreds of thousands of employees

00:19:43.074 --> 00:19:45.184
and they're in 42 countries or whatever,

00:19:45.184 --> 00:19:46.434
and going, huh.

00:19:48.633 --> 00:19:51.399
And so that's what I'm seeing now,

00:19:51.399 --> 00:19:54.399
as opposed to this suave, or shored,

00:19:55.372 --> 00:19:57.722
just we'll just keep moving along

00:19:57.722 --> 00:19:59.743
and we'll hire some more.

00:19:59.743 --> 00:20:02.113
Dusting of green washing on top

00:20:02.113 --> 00:20:04.072
to make sure that we take the press

00:20:04.072 --> 00:20:06.730
off our back or whatever, right?

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:07.563
Yeah, no.

00:20:07.563 --> 00:20:10.671
And so you're seeing companies like Nestle,

00:20:10.671 --> 00:20:14.462
which talk about a bad boy reputation 20 years ago

00:20:14.462 --> 00:20:16.141
for baby formula in Africa.

00:20:16.141 --> 00:20:17.141
I mean just,

00:20:18.511 --> 00:20:21.309
but they went through a couple more CEOs since then,

00:20:21.309 --> 00:20:25.413
who really, really cleaned up their act.

00:20:25.413 --> 00:20:30.009
They've sold off all their plastic water bottle companies.

00:20:30.009 --> 00:20:30.842
Probably the biggest

00:20:30.842 --> 00:20:33.874
single use plastic manufacturer in the world.

00:20:33.874 --> 00:20:34.707
Coca cola.

00:20:34.707 --> 00:20:39.682
But now, their slogan is generation regeneration

00:20:39.682 --> 00:20:44.682
and converting 600,000 farms to regenerative agriculture.

00:20:44.703 --> 00:20:46.552
And this is really interesting

00:20:46.552 --> 00:20:51.389
because you say, well, they're doing that to be nice, no.

00:20:51.389 --> 00:20:52.397
I'm not saying they're not nice.

00:20:52.397 --> 00:20:56.655
I'm just saying, are they doing that to be responsible?

00:20:56.655 --> 00:20:58.966
No, they wanna be responsible.

00:20:58.966 --> 00:21:03.668
They're doing it because they're an old line company

00:21:03.668 --> 00:21:05.868
they've been around for a long time.

00:21:05.868 --> 00:21:09.586
These farms, some of them go back five generations.

00:21:09.586 --> 00:21:11.525
They've been dealing with the farms for five generations,

00:21:11.525 --> 00:21:12.999
these are relationships,

00:21:12.999 --> 00:21:17.497
and these farms in south America and Africa

00:21:17.497 --> 00:21:20.164
for cacao and coffee are hurting

00:21:22.598 --> 00:21:26.388
and they're hurting from drought, from heat.

00:21:26.388 --> 00:21:29.471
And so regeneration is about ensuring

00:21:33.506 --> 00:21:35.017
the resilience and the longevity

00:21:35.017 --> 00:21:37.136
of those relationships and their supply chain.

00:21:37.136 --> 00:21:38.657
It's like an infrastructure bill.

00:21:38.657 --> 00:21:39.490
Right.

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:40.909
If they wanna continue doing what they're doing,

00:21:40.909 --> 00:21:42.657
They're gonna have to look at the soil

00:21:42.657 --> 00:21:45.617
and the wellbeing of the farm lands.

00:21:45.617 --> 00:21:47.788
Even the varieties, Arabica coffees,

00:21:47.788 --> 00:21:49.326
they're looking at wild varieties

00:21:49.326 --> 00:21:52.659
that can stand 5, 6, 7 degrees more heat

00:21:54.238 --> 00:21:55.857
than the extant ones

00:21:55.857 --> 00:21:57.718
and grafting those onto the trees

00:21:57.718 --> 00:22:00.719
and working with agroforestry and mixed cropping

00:22:00.719 --> 00:22:03.497
and all different ways to preserve water,

00:22:03.497 --> 00:22:05.119
