00:40:26.375 --> 00:40:28.917
to promote that regeneration

00:40:28.917 --> 00:40:31.335
so that it can become self perpetuating.

00:40:31.335 --> 00:40:33.616
Yeah. It's not getting out of the way so much.

00:40:33.616 --> 00:40:35.786
It's not thinking hierarchically

00:40:35.786 --> 00:40:38.077
that you know what needs to be done.

00:40:38.077 --> 00:40:39.746
It's a matter of doing,

00:40:39.746 --> 00:40:42.478
not knowing what needs to be done.

00:40:42.478 --> 00:40:45.537
And then that's what we've done up to now,

00:40:45.537 --> 00:40:46.370
this needs to be done.

00:40:46.370 --> 00:40:48.277
And all of this needs to be done.

00:40:48.277 --> 00:40:49.815
This needs to be done,

00:40:49.815 --> 00:40:52.616
that the people wanna heal themselves.

00:40:52.616 --> 00:40:55.365
They wanna heal the society,

00:40:55.365 --> 00:40:56.315
the places they live in,

00:40:56.315 --> 00:40:58.485
and they wanna heal the land.

00:40:58.485 --> 00:41:00.818
I was once in the Northwest,

00:41:02.615 --> 00:41:03.965
this is years ago now.

00:41:03.965 --> 00:41:07.428
But when, there are hanging spotted owls

00:41:07.428 --> 00:41:08.525
in the back of pickup trucks,

00:41:08.525 --> 00:41:11.283
cause there loggers and the newcomers

00:41:11.283 --> 00:41:13.932
is so to speak of from the cities

00:41:13.932 --> 00:41:16.205
where just at pun intended,

00:41:16.205 --> 00:41:19.493
I guess, loggerheads, and fighting.

00:41:19.493 --> 00:41:23.997
'cause the loggers felt that these city slickers

00:41:23.997 --> 00:41:28.747
or these liberals were tying to make them jobless, right?

00:41:29.957 --> 00:41:34.935
And I remember being in a city, was a town really,

00:41:34.935 --> 00:41:37.352
and to talk about the future.

00:41:38.516 --> 00:41:42.349
So I speak and the aisle is aisle on this side

00:41:43.546 --> 00:41:46.524
was all the loggers and the guys of the plaid shirts.

00:41:46.524 --> 00:41:49.441
And pickup trucks on this side was,

00:41:50.477 --> 00:41:52.693
the environmentalists and the conservationists

00:41:52.693 --> 00:41:55.107
and the people who really wanted to take care of everything.

00:41:55.107 --> 00:41:58.690
But I just ask simple questions and I said,

00:41:59.853 --> 00:42:03.555
how many people here want their rivers to have more fish

00:42:03.555 --> 00:42:05.972
or less fish in 30, 40 years?

00:42:07.445 --> 00:42:10.319
And everybody raised their hand like more,

00:42:10.319 --> 00:42:13.044
how many people want cleaner water, dirty water,

00:42:13.044 --> 00:42:16.077
and all always went out, not today,

00:42:16.077 --> 00:42:18.678
but 30, 40 years raise your hand.

00:42:18.678 --> 00:42:20.694
How many people would want their children if you have them

00:42:20.694 --> 00:42:22.907
to be able to stay here and get living wage jobs

00:42:22.907 --> 00:42:24.216
and stay close to the family,

00:42:24.216 --> 00:42:26.137
rather than having to go to the city?

00:42:26.137 --> 00:42:28.175
Everybody raised their hand.

00:42:28.175 --> 00:42:31.486
How many people want hunting to get better or worse?

00:42:31.486 --> 00:42:32.616
Everybody raise their hand.

00:42:32.616 --> 00:42:33.449
How many...

00:42:33.449 --> 00:42:34.386
just went on and on.

00:42:34.386 --> 00:42:37.820
I said, you're all in agreement.

00:42:37.820 --> 00:42:40.164
You agree on everything you want.

00:42:40.164 --> 00:42:41.586
So the question is,

00:42:41.586 --> 00:42:44.525
instead of talking about what you don't agree about

00:42:44.525 --> 00:42:48.135
is to work backwards from where you agree.

00:42:48.135 --> 00:42:49.814
But then it has to be about

00:42:49.814 --> 00:42:51.906
finding a way forward

00:42:51.906 --> 00:42:55.055
that isn't a threat or a perceived threat

00:42:55.055 --> 00:42:55.955
to people's livelihoods.

00:42:55.955 --> 00:42:56.788
Of course.

00:42:56.788 --> 00:42:57.847
Yeah, of course.

00:42:57.847 --> 00:42:58.866
Absolutely.

00:42:58.866 --> 00:43:01.246
So there has to be a just transition for coworkers,

00:43:01.246 --> 00:43:04.056
has to be a just transition for fossil fuel workers.

00:43:04.056 --> 00:43:06.656
There has to be compassionate.

00:43:06.656 --> 00:43:07.515
There has to be care.

00:43:07.515 --> 00:43:09.195
There has to be acknowledgement

00:43:09.195 --> 00:43:10.525
that these people are good people

00:43:10.525 --> 00:43:14.044
and they have families and they care about the world

00:43:14.044 --> 00:43:15.224
just as much as you do,

00:43:15.224 --> 00:43:16.528
they care about it a different way.

00:43:16.528 --> 00:43:17.872
They've been taught in a different way.

00:43:17.872 --> 00:43:19.395
They think in a different way.

00:43:19.395 --> 00:43:21.424
That doesn't make them bad.

00:43:21.424 --> 00:43:22.304
Doesn't make you good.

00:43:22.304 --> 00:43:24.367
And so absolutely.

00:43:24.367 --> 00:43:27.058
And that's what it means for us to come together.

00:43:27.058 --> 00:43:29.537
But the most powerful way you come together

00:43:29.537 --> 00:43:31.275
is to listen not to know.

00:43:31.275 --> 00:43:36.265
And so again, if you go at the climate as a know it all

00:43:36.265 --> 00:43:40.165
I know you don't listen up, it's gonna fail.

00:43:40.165 --> 00:43:41.071
Right.

00:43:41.071 --> 00:43:42.162
But if you look at it

00:43:42.162 --> 00:43:44.763
from the point of view of the inherent values,

00:43:44.763 --> 00:43:46.846
that we almost all uphold

00:43:47.831 --> 00:43:51.642
in terms of what it means to be safe,

00:43:51.642 --> 00:43:52.475
to be a family,

00:43:52.475 --> 00:43:55.316
to have children, to not have children.

00:43:55.316 --> 00:43:56.973
If that's your choice,

00:43:56.973 --> 00:43:58.837
to be a part of your community,

00:43:58.837 --> 00:44:01.837
to be respectful, we're in agreement

00:44:02.784 --> 00:44:05.617
and what the world wants is to me,

00:44:07.252 --> 00:44:09.002
and this is my belief

00:44:10.206 --> 00:44:13.825
is that we've created such meaningless employment

00:44:13.825 --> 00:44:15.254
for the bulk of humanity.

00:44:15.254 --> 00:44:17.677
I mean, they know it's meaningless,

00:44:17.677 --> 00:44:18.543
it's repetitive,

00:44:18.543 --> 00:44:20.506
repetitive, excuse me.

00:44:20.506 --> 00:44:21.673
It is kind of,

00:44:23.574 --> 00:44:26.823
it doesn't challenge them in terms of intelligence.

00:44:26.823 --> 00:44:28.546
It doesn't pay well,

00:44:28.546 --> 00:44:31.066
it's in poor working conditions.

00:44:31.066 --> 00:44:33.385
I'll give an example.

00:44:33.385 --> 00:44:37.468
I calculated how much a worker would make in 1800

00:44:39.117 --> 00:44:42.950
and the dark satanic hubs in mills of England.

00:44:44.045 --> 00:44:46.594
And these are textile mills.

00:44:46.594 --> 00:44:48.385
It's a penny an hour.

00:44:48.385 --> 00:44:49.218
Okay

00:44:49.218 --> 00:44:50.254
And today's dollars

00:44:50.254 --> 00:44:53.293
because the penny was related to golden silver.

00:44:53.293 --> 00:44:54.876
Then it's 30 cents.

00:44:55.772 --> 00:44:57.345
It was 30 cents an hour.

00:44:57.345 --> 00:44:59.644
At that time in today's dollars.

00:44:59.644 --> 00:45:03.045
The average pay today for garment workers

00:45:03.045 --> 00:45:06.264
in the world is 30 cents an hour.

00:45:06.264 --> 00:45:10.183
So, in 220 years we haven't changed the pay scale

00:45:10.183 --> 00:45:12.525
for garment workers at all.

00:45:12.525 --> 00:45:15.464
So what people are looking for

00:45:15.464 --> 00:45:18.493
is purpose and meaning and dignity.

00:45:18.493 --> 00:45:21.576
And there could be no more meaningful

00:45:23.078 --> 00:45:24.644
work in the world today,

00:45:24.644 --> 00:45:26.763
which is to restore life on earth.

00:45:26.763 --> 00:45:27.832
So the book is very much

00:45:27.832 --> 00:45:30.410
about all the different, fascinating ways

00:45:30.410 --> 00:45:32.160
that that can happen.

00:45:34.090 --> 00:45:37.763
And it has a demonstrative important impact

00:45:37.763 --> 00:45:40.091
on reversing global warming,

00:45:40.091 --> 00:45:41.885
as opposed to, oh, get solar,

00:45:41.885 --> 00:45:43.928
get an EV, do this too.

00:45:43.928 --> 00:45:46.761
I'm not saying you shouldn't do that if you can,

00:45:46.761 --> 00:45:50.070
but for most of humanity, those are not options.

00:45:50.070 --> 00:45:52.398
And so we have to look at it from a much broader,

00:45:52.398 --> 00:45:55.231
more enlarging, more upholding way

00:45:56.110 --> 00:45:58.658
that relates to what people can do.

00:45:58.658 --> 00:46:01.648
And the other thing I think it's important to do

00:46:01.648 --> 00:46:05.679
is to stop thinking that this is what you ought to do.

00:46:05.679 --> 00:46:07.180
These are the top 10 solutions.

00:46:07.180 --> 00:46:08.840
You should focus on those.

00:46:08.840 --> 00:46:11.658
That was another thing that Drawdown did inadvertently

00:46:11.658 --> 00:46:13.499
was his to rank all the solutions by impact.

00:46:13.499 --> 00:46:14.799
And then people said,

00:46:14.799 --> 00:46:17.609
all looked at the top 10 or the top 15

00:46:17.609 --> 00:46:22.359
and not to say that they aren't proportionally impactful.

00:46:23.350 --> 00:46:24.389
They are,

00:46:24.389 --> 00:46:26.920
but actually the most important things for you to do

00:46:26.920 --> 00:46:28.829
are the ones that light you up,

00:46:28.829 --> 00:46:30.110
where you just get excited.

00:46:30.110 --> 00:46:32.026
Nobody wants to wake up in the morning,

00:46:32.026 --> 00:46:34.161
and says I can't wait to mitigate today.

00:46:34.161 --> 00:46:36.655
Yeah. That was a similar message

00:46:36.655 --> 00:46:38.238
in Margaret's book,

00:46:40.307 --> 00:46:42.013
it was called like fighting the climate crisis

00:46:42.013 --> 00:46:42.846
or something like that.

00:46:42.846 --> 00:46:43.679
I don't know if you read that book.

00:46:43.679 --> 00:46:44.512
It's a good one.

00:46:44.512 --> 00:46:45.508
But I have her on the show.

00:46:45.508 --> 00:46:46.341
Yeah.

00:46:46.341 --> 00:46:48.012
And it's just how it's all about, like,

00:46:48.012 --> 00:46:49.492
how do you get people plugged into this?

00:46:49.492 --> 00:46:50.973
And you got to get them excited.

00:46:50.973 --> 00:46:53.599
And I think because we're so divorced

00:46:53.599 --> 00:46:56.658
from the natural cycle of life

00:46:56.658 --> 00:46:59.178
and the earth beneath our feet,

00:46:59.178 --> 00:47:01.700
finding a way to reconnect with that,

00:47:01.700 --> 00:47:05.371
that meets a certain person's enthusiasm

00:47:05.371 --> 00:47:06.989
is a great way forward.

00:47:06.989 --> 00:47:10.406
And it shifts the lens away from the idea

00:47:11.429 --> 00:47:14.140
that climate change is happening to us.

00:47:14.140 --> 00:47:15.850
To this idea that I know you've spoken about,

00:47:15.850 --> 00:47:19.249
which is the idea that climate change is happening for us.

00:47:19.249 --> 00:47:21.400
Like where is the opportunity here

00:47:21.400 --> 00:47:23.550
to reconfigure everything and create

00:47:23.550 --> 00:47:26.572
a completely different way of living

00:47:26.572 --> 00:47:30.989
for the vast majority of people who are so disengaged

00:47:31.880 --> 00:47:33.970
from the natural environment.

00:47:33.970 --> 00:47:37.053
Exactly. And the thing is if you...

00:47:39.618 --> 00:47:42.747
I mean, there's solutions there like beavers.

00:47:42.747 --> 00:47:43.627
I know I love that.

00:47:43.627 --> 00:47:45.287
I didn't know all that about beaver, right?

00:47:45.287 --> 00:47:48.555
It's like, if that excites you and it does,

00:47:48.555 --> 00:47:51.469
by the way, are people just love beavers.

00:47:51.469 --> 00:47:52.302
They studied them, they work.

00:47:52.302 --> 00:47:53.775
And they tried to,

00:47:53.775 --> 00:47:55.257
I didn't realize how many beavers there were.

00:47:55.257 --> 00:47:56.795
There are a lot of beavers

00:47:56.795 --> 00:47:59.437
and who trapped them in the cities

00:47:59.437 --> 00:48:03.625
and then not to take them where they will do more good,

00:48:03.625 --> 00:48:05.718
upstream, so to speak

00:48:05.718 --> 00:48:08.507
in mountainous areas and so forth.

00:48:08.507 --> 00:48:12.840
But if you doing something that just you love to do,

00:48:14.208 --> 00:48:17.937
you're very likely then to be open to other things

00:48:17.937 --> 00:48:19.438
that need to be done

00:48:19.438 --> 00:48:22.648
because now you're starting to see yourself

00:48:22.648 --> 00:48:24.216
as an integral part,

00:48:24.216 --> 00:48:26.706
an effective integral part

00:48:26.706 --> 00:48:29.616
to restarting life on earth,

00:48:29.616 --> 00:48:32.476
which is really what reversing global warming is about.

00:48:32.476 --> 00:48:33.815
It's about restoring life on earth.

00:48:33.815 --> 00:48:35.805
We've taken as much as we should,

00:48:35.805 --> 00:48:38.577
long time ago actually, we've passed that limit.

00:48:38.577 --> 00:48:40.494
And so again, you know,

00:48:42.347 --> 00:48:44.106
I define regeneration

00:48:44.106 --> 00:48:47.099
as putting life at the center very act and decision.

00:48:47.099 --> 00:48:49.446
I mean, that's what regeneration is.

00:48:49.446 --> 00:48:51.246
And so looking through that lens,

00:48:51.246 --> 00:48:52.096
looking through that...

00:48:52.096 --> 00:48:54.179
seeing the world that way

00:48:55.339 --> 00:48:58.422
then allows you to basically reflect,

00:49:04.107 --> 00:49:05.345
on what it is you're doing

00:49:05.345 --> 00:49:08.226
and what it is that you're a part of this being done

00:49:08.226 --> 00:49:09.697
and how it's being done.

00:49:09.697 --> 00:49:11.477
And it doesn't mean leaving it or quitting.

00:49:11.477 --> 00:49:13.045
It means can we change that?

00:49:13.045 --> 00:49:14.127
Can we change that?

00:49:14.127 --> 00:49:15.215
Have you thought about this?

00:49:15.215 --> 00:49:16.707
Is this possibility?

00:49:16.707 --> 00:49:17.578
Is there this way,

00:49:17.578 --> 00:49:22.578
can we, it's really about doing what you can in place,

00:49:22.886 --> 00:49:26.378
as opposed to finding some other place in the world

00:49:26.378 --> 00:49:27.873
where you can make a difference.

00:49:27.873 --> 00:49:30.706
you're most effective where people know and respect you.

00:49:30.706 --> 00:49:32.067
Yeah.

00:49:32.067 --> 00:49:33.985
When you say the word regeneration

00:49:33.985 --> 00:49:36.577
immediately what comes to mind for me

00:49:36.577 --> 00:49:38.139
is soil regeneration.

00:49:38.139 --> 00:49:40.899
And, there's been a massive explosion

00:49:40.899 --> 00:49:43.099
in terms of people's awareness

00:49:43.099 --> 00:49:44.945
and care with respect to

00:49:44.945 --> 00:49:46.626
how we're treating our soils.

00:49:46.626 --> 00:49:50.325
And, you can credit our friend Reiland

00:49:50.325 --> 00:49:52.166
for all the beautiful work that he's doing

00:49:52.166 --> 00:49:54.846
with kiss the ground and the documentary that he made,

00:49:54.846 --> 00:49:57.205
John and Molly Chester and the biggest little farm,

00:49:57.205 --> 00:50:00.857
like there are pieces of really powerful mass media

00:50:00.857 --> 00:50:03.432
that I think are shifting the paradigm

00:50:03.432 --> 00:50:05.544
and helping people to better understand

00:50:05.544 --> 00:50:07.627
this piece of the puzzle.

00:50:09.048 --> 00:50:11.537
But the book really canvases

00:50:11.537 --> 00:50:16.275
a much broader definition of what regeneration is

00:50:16.275 --> 00:50:18.446
and you break it down into all these different categories.

00:50:18.446 --> 00:50:19.777
You've got oceans and forests

00:50:19.777 --> 00:50:22.698
and wilding and land and people and cities

00:50:22.698 --> 00:50:24.617
and food and energy and industry.

00:50:24.617 --> 00:50:25.676
And within that,

00:50:25.676 --> 00:50:26.753
all these subcategories

00:50:26.753 --> 00:50:29.097
where people are doing amazing things.

00:50:29.097 --> 00:50:32.195
And you kind of explain why this is important,

00:50:32.195 --> 00:50:33.585
how you can get involved

00:50:33.585 --> 00:50:36.585
the new kind of operating modalities

00:50:37.603 --> 00:50:39.306
that are helping to heal the planet

00:50:39.306 --> 00:50:41.353
in these various capacities.

00:50:41.353 --> 00:50:44.458
And I think if you page through the book,

00:50:44.458 --> 00:50:46.326
you're gonna find that thing

00:50:46.326 --> 00:50:48.498
that's gonna make you enthusiastic,

00:50:48.498 --> 00:50:52.264
whether it's beavers or, urban gardening or whatever it is.

00:50:52.264 --> 00:50:53.186
And what was interesting is

00:50:53.186 --> 00:50:54.615
there were so many things in here.

00:50:54.615 --> 00:50:56.826
Like, I feel like I have a pretty good grip

00:50:56.826 --> 00:50:59.184
on this general landscape,

00:50:59.184 --> 00:51:00.346
but there was a lot in here

00:51:00.346 --> 00:51:01.565
that I didn't know anything about

00:51:01.565 --> 00:51:03.986
some pretty interesting things in here.

00:51:03.986 --> 00:51:07.008
Like I wrote down a few of them,

00:51:07.008 --> 00:51:11.925
like the Azzola Fern was one that I didn't know much about.

00:51:12.824 --> 00:51:15.637
Maybe you can explain that a little bit,

00:51:15.637 --> 00:51:18.970
trophic cascades, what are these things?

00:51:22.608 --> 00:51:27.358
They are and when you first see them on the page going,

00:51:28.715 --> 00:51:30.828
what does this have to do with...?

00:51:30.828 --> 00:51:34.805
I'm worried about the future, my children,

00:51:34.805 --> 00:51:36.917
and he's talking about a trophic cascade.

00:51:36.917 --> 00:51:38.552
He's talking about the beavers.

00:51:38.552 --> 00:51:39.385
Yeah.

00:51:39.385 --> 00:51:40.327
Same with beavers.

00:51:40.327 --> 00:51:43.327
Azzola Fern is a Fern and it floats,

00:51:46.414 --> 00:51:49.905
it lives on the surface of the water.

00:51:49.905 --> 00:51:52.988
It metabolizes nitrogen from the air.

00:51:54.744 --> 00:51:57.325
It fixes nitrogen from the air.

00:51:57.325 --> 00:52:00.575
So it doubles every two and a half days

00:52:03.603 --> 00:52:05.704
if it's cold, in two days,

00:52:05.704 --> 00:52:07.025
if it's warm, in three days,

00:52:07.025 --> 00:52:09.442
but approximately it doubles.

00:52:10.685 --> 00:52:13.268
And it is rich in omega threes,

00:52:15.396 --> 00:52:17.834
which is unusual for a plant

00:52:17.834 --> 00:52:20.751
and needed by this omega six world.

00:52:21.595 --> 00:52:23.836
We live in a junk food

00:52:23.836 --> 00:52:27.669
and it makes the oil and it sequesters carbon.

00:52:33.452 --> 00:52:34.404
And the reason we know that,

00:52:34.404 --> 00:52:37.757
not that any plant doesn't do that,

00:52:37.757 --> 00:52:41.355
every plant does, but there was an Azolla event,

00:52:41.355 --> 00:52:43.435
49 million years ago.

00:52:43.435 --> 00:52:44.367
And at that time,

00:52:44.367 --> 00:52:46.617
that PPM of CO2 was 25,000.

00:52:49.105 --> 00:52:54.105
It was pretty hot and there was no ice in the Arctic

00:52:54.126 --> 00:52:56.543
and it was a freshwater lens.

00:52:58.228 --> 00:53:01.037
And you had these Azzola it's called the Azolla event,

00:53:01.037 --> 00:53:03.957
but there's all events where in the summer it would be

00:53:03.957 --> 00:53:05.707
covered with Azzola.

00:53:05.707 --> 00:53:07.989
And then in the cooler part in the winter,

00:53:07.989 --> 00:53:10.989
it would die and sink to the bottom.

00:53:12.218 --> 00:53:13.248
And then that happened.

00:53:13.248 --> 00:53:16.566
And within a relatively short geological time,

00:53:16.566 --> 00:53:19.649
it went down to from 25 to 6,000 PPM.

