01:06:17.386 --> 01:06:19.386
That's really important.

01:06:20.527 --> 01:06:22.878
There is, as opposed to thinking, well,

01:06:22.878 --> 01:06:23.958
it doesn't really make a difference.

01:06:23.958 --> 01:06:25.067
It makes a huge difference.

01:06:25.067 --> 01:06:27.728
And those are the differences,

01:06:27.728 --> 01:06:28.747
all around the world.

01:06:28.747 --> 01:06:30.738
And I guess my question is,

01:06:30.738 --> 01:06:33.348
how do we get that going?

01:06:33.348 --> 01:06:34.181
Right?

01:06:34.181 --> 01:06:37.237
I mean that's the $64,000 question, right?

01:06:37.237 --> 01:06:39.980
Like I would suspect that most farmers

01:06:39.980 --> 01:06:41.489
would rather be farming

01:06:41.489 --> 01:06:43.277
in the way that John and Molly are farming

01:06:43.277 --> 01:06:45.065
than the way that they're currently farming.

01:06:45.065 --> 01:06:45.898
Yes.

01:06:45.898 --> 01:06:47.547
But the gap from status quo

01:06:47.547 --> 01:06:50.864
to the situation that John and Molly are in

01:06:50.864 --> 01:06:52.408
is pretty vast.

01:06:52.408 --> 01:06:55.819
You have to create some kind of incentivization

01:06:55.819 --> 01:06:58.756
or transitionary situation

01:06:58.756 --> 01:07:01.339
to allow those people to free themselves

01:07:01.339 --> 01:07:03.798
from the kind of tyrannical situation that they're in,

01:07:03.798 --> 01:07:04.777
where they're in this

01:07:04.777 --> 01:07:08.259
basically an indentured servitude relationship

01:07:08.259 --> 01:07:10.607
with big agricultural firms

01:07:10.607 --> 01:07:12.938
that compel them to do things in a certain way

01:07:12.938 --> 01:07:15.355
and allow them to transition.

01:07:16.247 --> 01:07:19.286
Because, if you get anything from that documentary,

01:07:19.286 --> 01:07:20.258
it's how long it took

01:07:20.258 --> 01:07:21.938
to get it to a situation

01:07:21.938 --> 01:07:24.126
where now it's semi self perpetuating.

01:07:24.126 --> 01:07:26.623
There were some lean, very difficult years

01:07:26.623 --> 01:07:28.007
where most people would have bailed

01:07:28.007 --> 01:07:30.095
and gone back to the old ways.

01:07:30.095 --> 01:07:31.825
So you have to create an economic buffer

01:07:31.825 --> 01:07:34.492
for these people and an on-ramp.

01:07:37.133 --> 01:07:41.347
The way to understand industrial ag versus say,

01:07:41.347 --> 01:07:46.244
not opposed to, but compared to regenerative ag is simple

01:07:46.244 --> 01:07:49.077
and industrial agriculture started

01:07:50.026 --> 01:07:53.218
or really got going in the middle of the 19th century

01:07:53.218 --> 01:07:56.515
when chemical fertilizers were invented

01:07:56.515 --> 01:07:59.529
and then got nos on steroids.

01:07:59.529 --> 01:08:01.958
Once the Haber Bosch method

01:08:01.958 --> 01:08:05.977
of creating nitrates was early 20th century.

01:08:05.977 --> 01:08:10.060
And so industrial ag basically feeds plants, NPK,

01:08:15.248 --> 01:08:19.081
and then the macro nutrients it needs to grow.

01:08:20.986 --> 01:08:23.855
And when it first started to be used in the 19th century,

01:08:23.855 --> 01:08:27.737
there was a lot of bad farming in Germany and Europe.

01:08:27.737 --> 01:08:30.916
There was hunger issues there really were.

01:08:30.916 --> 01:08:35.249
And all of a sudden you put this powder on the field

01:08:37.377 --> 01:08:40.157
and these bright green plants

01:08:40.157 --> 01:08:41.977
that are taller than the ones you didn't get

01:08:41.977 --> 01:08:43.937
and go, hey, we're onto something.

01:08:43.937 --> 01:08:45.327
I mean, this is cool.

01:08:45.327 --> 01:08:47.568
And it just got better and better quote unquote,

01:08:47.568 --> 01:08:51.367
in a sense of phosphorus and potassium,

01:08:51.367 --> 01:08:55.117
and pretty soon that was how you grew plants.

01:08:57.088 --> 01:08:58.086
You could grow more of it.

01:08:58.086 --> 01:09:00.138
That yields are greater, et cetera.

01:09:00.138 --> 01:09:01.785
It always takes time.

01:09:01.785 --> 01:09:03.985
When you do that to realize

01:09:03.985 --> 01:09:05.299
that the plants are weaker

01:09:05.299 --> 01:09:08.326
because regenerative ag actually doesn't feed plants,

01:09:08.326 --> 01:09:09.493
it feeds soil.

01:09:10.386 --> 01:09:13.856
And for the 400 million years that plants have been here

01:09:13.856 --> 01:09:16.439
soil has always fed the plants.

01:09:17.626 --> 01:09:20.087
And so in essentially industrial ag

01:09:20.087 --> 01:09:22.703
is putting plants on an IV drip,

01:09:22.703 --> 01:09:24.672
that's what it's doing.

01:09:24.672 --> 01:09:26.962
And you take the drip away and the plants suffer

01:09:26.962 --> 01:09:28.573
because the soil has been degraded

01:09:28.573 --> 01:09:31.421
and it's really holding the plant up.

01:09:31.421 --> 01:09:32.941
It's not feeding the plant.

01:09:32.941 --> 01:09:34.442
So there is that transition.

01:09:34.442 --> 01:09:36.021
There's no question about it.

01:09:36.021 --> 01:09:39.354
And we do need a way to support farmers,

01:09:40.494 --> 01:09:45.494
because the reason I have never met a regen ag farmer,

01:09:45.741 --> 01:09:47.400
and I've met quite a number

01:09:47.400 --> 01:09:51.233
who did it because it was a right thing to do.

01:09:52.292 --> 01:09:54.432
Now, you say, whoa, why did they do it?

01:09:54.432 --> 01:09:56.722
They did it because they hit the wall.

01:09:56.722 --> 01:09:58.658
It's the same as people's health.

01:09:58.658 --> 01:10:01.281
What people don't change their diet

01:10:01.281 --> 01:10:03.322
because it's a right thing to do.

01:10:03.322 --> 01:10:05.501
They change it cause they hit the wall and said,

01:10:05.501 --> 01:10:07.261
this is not working at all.

01:10:07.261 --> 01:10:09.214
And then they turn to people

01:10:09.214 --> 01:10:13.341
to functional medicine, to you, to whatever.

01:10:13.341 --> 01:10:15.081
I mean, they turn into their,

01:10:15.081 --> 01:10:15.914
wait a minute.

01:10:15.914 --> 01:10:16.832
I don't know what to do.

01:10:16.832 --> 01:10:18.821
And so the farmers I've met,

01:10:18.821 --> 01:10:20.950
basically hit the wall.

01:10:20.950 --> 01:10:21.950
They were running out of money

01:10:21.950 --> 01:10:24.690
and they were feeling this squeeze

01:10:24.690 --> 01:10:27.241
between producing commodities,

01:10:27.241 --> 01:10:31.020
having the prices out of their control

01:10:31.020 --> 01:10:34.660
and being part of an over-producing system,

01:10:34.660 --> 01:10:37.150
then suppress prices and their costs were going up

01:10:37.150 --> 01:10:40.123
because the soil was getting poor, poor,

01:10:40.123 --> 01:10:42.122
turning into dirt as has been said,

01:10:42.122 --> 01:10:44.061
and then it needed more and more and more input.

01:10:44.061 --> 01:10:45.692
And it needed pesticides

01:10:45.692 --> 01:10:47.028
because the plants were so weak,

01:10:47.028 --> 01:10:47.861
the bugs were eating it

01:10:47.861 --> 01:10:49.152
and then it needed glyphosate

01:10:49.152 --> 01:10:51.930
because the weeds could out-compete the plant.

01:10:51.930 --> 01:10:55.178
And pretty soon you have basically modern ag.

01:10:55.178 --> 01:10:57.070
But again, as I said earlier,

01:10:57.070 --> 01:10:58.443
that was, farmers could see

01:10:58.443 --> 01:11:01.421
that that degenerative road ended,

01:11:01.421 --> 01:11:02.453
they could see the ending,

01:11:02.453 --> 01:11:05.703
it was called basically the bank saying

01:11:06.690 --> 01:11:10.419
pay up or we're gonna take all your equipment.

01:11:10.419 --> 01:11:11.610
Right, right. Yeah.

01:11:11.610 --> 01:11:12.830
And that's right.

01:11:12.830 --> 01:11:15.512
So the biggest fact that

01:11:15.512 --> 01:11:18.081
the biggest change in the farming community

01:11:18.081 --> 01:11:20.838
on regen ag is caused by farmers

01:11:20.838 --> 01:11:22.402
that farmers talking to farmers.

01:11:22.402 --> 01:11:23.235
Right.

01:11:23.235 --> 01:11:24.068
That's what I was thinking

01:11:24.068 --> 01:11:26.672
like pivoting from that blame and shame,

01:11:26.672 --> 01:11:30.589
modus operandi into empowerment and engagement.

01:11:31.632 --> 01:11:34.219
It's those farmers who have made that transition,

01:11:34.219 --> 01:11:35.133
who are the ones

01:11:35.133 --> 01:11:36.448
who are gonna be able to communicate

01:11:36.448 --> 01:11:37.493
to the other farmers there.

01:11:37.493 --> 01:11:39.118
They're not gonna want to hear it from you,

01:11:39.118 --> 01:11:40.451
or let alone me.

01:11:41.560 --> 01:11:43.623
I mean, God forbid, so yeah,

01:11:43.623 --> 01:11:46.790
like creating community around that,

01:11:46.790 --> 01:11:49.062
that's supportive and empowering

01:11:49.062 --> 01:11:51.053
to helping people make that transition.

01:11:51.053 --> 01:11:52.873
But also on top of that

01:11:52.873 --> 01:11:55.301
systems that are conducive to that

01:11:55.301 --> 01:11:57.720
being an economic viability.

01:11:57.720 --> 01:11:59.720
But there are things happening

01:11:59.720 --> 01:12:03.984
where the demand for regenerative to duly produce

01:12:03.984 --> 01:12:07.433
seeds, grains, and obviously meat as well,

01:12:07.433 --> 01:12:11.762
but are increasing faster than production.

01:12:11.762 --> 01:12:12.595
Sure.

01:12:12.595 --> 01:12:15.762
And so farmers are seeing a premium,

01:12:17.144 --> 01:12:19.477
their inputs are going down,

01:12:20.394 --> 01:12:21.274
their costs are going down,

01:12:21.274 --> 01:12:24.191
but actually the income is going up

01:12:25.501 --> 01:12:29.334
and you see 10X differences in profit per acre

01:12:31.329 --> 01:12:32.480
for regen farmers.

01:12:32.480 --> 01:12:35.801
And as Gay Brown famously said,

01:12:35.801 --> 01:12:39.731
he said, I got tired of basically signing

01:12:39.731 --> 01:12:40.834
the front of the check.

01:12:40.834 --> 01:12:43.472
And now I sign the back of the check,

01:12:43.472 --> 01:12:47.942
notice it shifted from an expense to income,

01:12:47.942 --> 01:12:50.920
and you don't go back from that.

01:12:50.920 --> 01:12:55.152
And so now there's companies and processes

01:12:55.152 --> 01:12:57.543
and techniques and satellites,

01:12:57.543 --> 01:13:00.504
and there is a lot of support now

01:13:00.504 --> 01:13:04.263
being created for regen ag in its true form.

01:13:04.263 --> 01:13:05.791
And it's being also done

01:13:05.791 --> 01:13:08.150
in some dubious forms as well.

01:13:08.150 --> 01:13:09.171
Yeah.
Yeah.

01:13:09.171 --> 01:13:10.721
And yet, I just,

01:13:10.721 --> 01:13:12.781
I was out of town the other day

01:13:12.781 --> 01:13:14.941
and just i was in an airplane yesterday

01:13:14.941 --> 01:13:15.862
and you look down

01:13:15.862 --> 01:13:19.531
and you don't see a lot of regenerative farms.

01:13:19.531 --> 01:13:24.313
You see giant swaths of square and round parcels

01:13:24.313 --> 01:13:25.968
Headed irrigation.
Yeah.

01:13:25.968 --> 01:13:27.862
Yeah, so where are we

01:13:27.862 --> 01:13:29.372
in terms of that becoming

01:13:29.372 --> 01:13:32.352
like reaching some kind of inflection point with this?

01:13:32.352 --> 01:13:34.103
I don't know. I don't know.

01:13:34.103 --> 01:13:36.186
I know that like the big,

01:13:38.251 --> 01:13:40.477
big companies are pivoting on this.

01:13:40.477 --> 01:13:42.849
They have the same questions

01:13:42.849 --> 01:13:46.980
which is, how do we make the transition?

01:13:46.980 --> 01:13:47.813
They don't,

01:13:47.813 --> 01:13:49.472
how did their farmers make the transition?

01:13:49.472 --> 01:13:50.822
And how's the brushes doing with rice

01:13:50.822 --> 01:13:54.155
general mills is trying to do with oats.

01:13:56.567 --> 01:13:57.567
But so many,

01:13:58.952 --> 01:14:01.085
like the clothing companies now

01:14:01.085 --> 01:14:04.983
are basically looking for regenerative fiber,

01:14:04.983 --> 01:14:07.821
primarily cotton, but other fibers as well.

01:14:07.821 --> 01:14:11.461
So you're seeing a supply demand dance.

01:14:11.461 --> 01:14:12.404
The consumer demand.

01:14:12.404 --> 01:14:14.912
I mean, I feel like Gen Z,

01:14:14.912 --> 01:14:16.221
the younger generation,

01:14:16.221 --> 01:14:19.211
they're up to speed on this stuff.

01:14:19.211 --> 01:14:20.874
And they're much more conscious

01:14:20.874 --> 01:14:23.132
about their consumer choices than our generation.

01:14:23.132 --> 01:14:26.881
Much more, I mean, when I started Erewhon,

01:14:26.881 --> 01:14:29.251
we are mom out all the time about you hippies

01:14:29.251 --> 01:14:32.334
you wanna feed the world organically,

01:14:33.543 --> 01:14:37.804
but we industrial ag have we take care of the children,

01:14:37.804 --> 01:14:38.925
we have a world of feed.

01:14:38.925 --> 01:14:43.925
You guys are sort of, self-indulgent almost narcissistic,

01:14:44.170 --> 01:14:48.674
and you're wanting things without chemicals and pesticides,

01:14:48.674 --> 01:14:51.701
and now I don't know how big the industry is

01:14:51.701 --> 01:14:53.761
it's so big that you can't get enough organic food

01:14:53.761 --> 01:14:55.193
in the United States yet.

01:14:55.193 --> 01:15:00.162
But the same thing is regen is not gonna go through that

01:15:00.162 --> 01:15:02.232
because you're seeing

01:15:02.232 --> 01:15:05.820
every major agricultural company in the world,

01:15:05.820 --> 01:15:10.737
Syngenta and Cartiva and Monsanto buyer and ADM and Cargill

01:15:12.242 --> 01:15:14.123
moving to regeneration.

01:15:14.123 --> 01:15:15.461
Now, I mean,

01:15:15.461 --> 01:15:18.939
keep your tongue firmly in your cheek on that one,

01:15:18.939 --> 01:15:23.524
because I feel like there's some co-ops going on there

01:15:23.524 --> 01:15:25.713
in terms of the term and the word,

01:15:25.713 --> 01:15:29.282
but as Franklin Roosevelt once said

01:15:29.282 --> 01:15:31.422
that sometimes the way you start telling the truth

01:15:31.422 --> 01:15:33.581
is by being a hypocrite at first.

01:15:33.581 --> 01:15:36.890
And so who knows where that's gonna go,

01:15:36.890 --> 01:15:38.463
but you do see indigo ag,

01:15:38.463 --> 01:15:40.701
which is all about regenerative farming

01:15:40.701 --> 01:15:42.899
from the get-go and not about chemicals,

01:15:42.899 --> 01:15:45.752
kind of like the opposite of a Monsanto

01:15:45.752 --> 01:15:48.380
doing really, really well.

01:15:48.380 --> 01:15:51.211
Working with not only with regenerative farmers,

01:15:51.211 --> 01:15:53.832
but in terms of, metrics

01:15:53.832 --> 01:15:57.961
in terms of measuring carbon and de commodification,

01:15:57.961 --> 01:16:02.072
that is connecting farmers directly to the buyers

01:16:02.072 --> 01:16:05.933
and getting the farmers out of the commodities business,

01:16:05.933 --> 01:16:08.824
where no matter how well you do,

01:16:08.824 --> 01:16:11.842
you're still, the price is the same for everybody.

01:16:11.842 --> 01:16:14.009
And so that has to happen.

01:16:15.222 --> 01:16:18.602
So that regenerative farmers can get a premium

01:16:18.602 --> 01:16:21.034
and that the food companies that buy that

01:16:21.034 --> 01:16:23.002
have a story and a narrative,

01:16:23.002 --> 01:16:24.878
that means something to Gen Z

01:16:24.878 --> 01:16:25.893
and everybody else who cares.

01:16:25.893 --> 01:16:29.037
Yeah. The story narrative piece, I think is big.

01:16:29.037 --> 01:16:32.912
I think most companies these days need something,

01:16:32.912 --> 01:16:36.495
there has to be a genuine, authentic aspect

01:16:38.264 --> 01:16:40.223
of doing business.

01:16:40.223 --> 01:16:41.878
Oh yeah. I mean,

01:16:41.878 --> 01:16:44.132
and I think they recognize that

01:16:44.132 --> 01:16:46.221
and I mean, we'll see what happens,

01:16:46.221 --> 01:16:50.888
but I think that we're in a period of tumultuous change.

01:16:52.042 --> 01:16:56.042
Some of it is threatening for sure and daunting.

01:16:58.720 --> 01:17:01.594
But I get the sense in the business community

01:17:01.594 --> 01:17:03.844
that the old ways are over.

01:17:06.412 --> 01:17:07.245
I really do.

01:17:07.245 --> 01:17:08.522
And I'm not praising anybody.

01:17:08.522 --> 01:17:12.104
I'm not to think finally business gets it.

01:17:12.104 --> 01:17:13.241
And I'm not saying that either,

01:17:13.241 --> 01:17:15.182
if anybody doesn't get it,

01:17:15.182 --> 01:17:17.560
I would say, it's the investors.

01:17:17.560 --> 01:17:19.633
They still want to put their money

01:17:19.633 --> 01:17:21.612
where they can get the highest return

01:17:21.612 --> 01:17:24.802
and the earth doesn't work that way.

01:17:24.802 --> 01:17:25.635
Right?

01:17:25.635 --> 01:17:28.131
Yeah. I mean, banking is a whole section in the book

01:17:28.131 --> 01:17:28.964
about that.

01:17:28.964 --> 01:17:30.047
Yeah, yeah.

01:17:33.422 --> 01:17:37.544
Where do you see the role of hydroponics in all of this?

01:17:37.544 --> 01:17:39.532
I mean, obviously this is that's growing plants

01:17:39.532 --> 01:17:41.370
in a manner that's divorced

01:17:41.370 --> 01:17:45.170
from the soil conversation all together.

01:17:45.170 --> 01:17:47.981
I suspect there's a great place for that

01:17:47.981 --> 01:17:51.193
in urban centers or places where there isn't soil,

01:17:51.193 --> 01:17:55.526
but how does that fit into the idea of regeneration?

01:17:56.443 --> 01:17:57.276
Well, it's interesting

01:17:57.276 --> 01:17:59.109
because plants suffer.

01:18:01.370 --> 01:18:02.921
that is to say,

01:18:02.921 --> 01:18:06.338
that they suffer because it gets too hot.

01:18:07.420 --> 01:18:09.773
It's too much sun, insects.

01:18:09.773 --> 01:18:13.773
They suffer for a lack of nutrient, for example.

01:18:14.783 --> 01:18:17.023
So they put the roots down deeper,

01:18:17.023 --> 01:18:19.452
the complexity of the taste

01:18:19.452 --> 01:18:21.411
and the phyto nutrients

01:18:21.411 --> 01:18:25.244
that are in plants are actually due to stress.

01:18:26.262 --> 01:18:29.954
So plants that are stressed are more nutritious.

01:18:29.954 --> 01:18:31.283
Right.

01:18:31.283 --> 01:18:33.623
When you do hydroponics,

01:18:33.623 --> 01:18:35.893
you're actually going back to industrial ag,

01:18:35.893 --> 01:18:37.210
which is, you're doing

01:18:37.210 --> 01:18:39.451
your as an IV drip into the roots.

01:18:39.451 --> 01:18:43.701
It's fine if you just Basil for salad or watercress

01:18:44.603 --> 01:18:48.394
or something for greenery in cities,

01:18:48.394 --> 01:18:52.044
the idea that that's going to produce nutritious food,

01:18:52.044 --> 01:18:54.033
I think is faulty

01:18:54.033 --> 01:18:58.143
because we have an nutritious food now everywhere.

01:18:58.143 --> 01:19:01.635
And we have food that comes from industrial ag.

01:19:01.635 --> 01:19:04.421
Both, whether it's row crops

01:19:04.421 --> 01:19:09.051
or in vegetables or whether it's in grains and seeds.

01:19:09.051 --> 01:19:10.743
And we have measured,

01:19:10.743 --> 01:19:12.084
we can measure the difference

01:19:12.084 --> 01:19:14.284
in the phytonutrients and minerals

01:19:14.284 --> 01:19:16.832
or the lack of minerals that are in these foods.

01:19:16.832 --> 01:19:19.732
So we haven't been able to invent a way

01:19:19.732 --> 01:19:24.353
to make a really super nutritious food without soil yet.

01:19:24.353 --> 01:19:25.354
And I don't think we ever will.

01:19:25.354 --> 01:19:28.222
What about the carbon sequestration piece of it?

01:19:28.222 --> 01:19:29.055
Of what?

01:19:30.021 --> 01:19:32.912
Meaning that if you're an urban centers

01:19:32.912 --> 01:19:33.925
and you don't have soil,

01:19:33.925 --> 01:19:36.401
but you have all these hydroponic gardens everywhere,

01:19:36.401 --> 01:19:38.052
is that moving the needle at all

01:19:38.052 --> 01:19:39.602
in terms of probably carbon?

01:19:39.602 --> 01:19:41.422
Probably not. I think it's...

01:19:41.422 --> 01:19:43.251
at the same time,

01:19:43.251 --> 01:19:45.323
we do wanna localize food production.

01:19:45.323 --> 01:19:47.221
We do wanna bring food into the city,

01:19:47.221 --> 01:19:51.131
into the peri urban areas, right around the city.

01:19:51.131 --> 01:19:52.800
And that does involve greenhouses

01:19:52.800 --> 01:19:57.052
that does involve all sorts of really ingenious techniques,

01:19:57.052 --> 01:20:01.612
to extend the season and to produce crops that people want.

01:20:01.612 --> 01:20:03.603
And there's nothing wrong

01:20:03.603 --> 01:20:08.163
with the crops that are grown hydroponically in terms of,

01:20:08.163 --> 01:20:10.000
but just not the idea

01:20:10.000 --> 01:20:13.622
that somehow that's going to replace the foods we need.

01:20:13.622 --> 01:20:15.143
They just won't,

01:20:15.143 --> 01:20:18.092
but it can produce very fresh and tasty.

01:20:18.092 --> 01:20:21.384
Like I said, parsley and basil and watercress and lettuces

01:20:21.384 --> 01:20:22.893
and things like that,

01:20:22.893 --> 01:20:26.326
which are not really, I mean, the major source

01:20:26.326 --> 01:20:28.659
of our nurture or nutrients.

01:20:30.177 --> 01:20:31.376
Let's talk about energy.

01:20:31.376 --> 01:20:32.427
Yeah.

01:20:32.427 --> 01:20:35.126
Why can't we get wind power?

01:20:35.126 --> 01:20:38.159
Like just rocking out everywhere.

01:20:38.159 --> 01:20:42.576
Like what are the impediments to really scaling that?

01:20:43.485 --> 01:20:44.318
Cause it seems to me

01:20:44.318 --> 01:20:48.568
that's the most viable best solution at the moment.

01:20:49.478 --> 01:20:52.256
I think the major impediment is sighting.

01:20:52.256 --> 01:20:53.937
In other words,

01:20:53.937 --> 01:20:56.024
people don't want

01:20:56.024 --> 01:20:58.274
people generally don't live

01:20:59.176 --> 01:21:02.247
where there is the high wind regimes.

01:21:02.247 --> 01:21:06.749
The Midwest is the winds blowing all the time, almost,

01:21:06.749 --> 01:21:09.332
and so their farmers love them,

01:21:12.746 --> 01:21:14.577
with their corn, they have two crops,

01:21:14.577 --> 01:21:16.160
kilowatts and corn.

01:21:17.685 --> 01:21:21.768
I think that the major obstacle is offshore wind.

01:21:23.987 --> 01:21:25.667
In other words, sighting offshore wind.

01:21:25.667 --> 01:21:28.000
And I think Heinrich, Starzl

01:21:29.127 --> 01:21:32.710
has Danish engineer has solved the problem.

01:21:36.389 --> 01:21:37.806
And he was the...

01:21:39.614 --> 01:21:40.447
when he was young,

01:21:40.447 --> 01:21:43.516
he started filling out with wind turbines.

01:21:43.516 --> 01:21:47.657
And the three blade configuration that you see today

01:21:47.657 --> 01:21:50.498
on the big turbines, he invented that

01:21:50.498 --> 01:21:53.998
and a farming supply company called Bestos

01:21:56.399 --> 01:21:57.888
heard about him.

01:21:57.888 --> 01:22:00.566
Because at that time farms had windmills.

01:22:00.566 --> 01:22:03.458
My grandfather's farm had a windmill on it for water.

01:22:03.458 --> 01:22:04.291
Right.

01:22:04.291 --> 01:22:07.874
And they bought the rights to his design.

01:22:08.748 --> 01:22:11.817
And Bestos is the biggest wind turbine company

01:22:11.817 --> 01:22:12.766
in the world today, 13 billion a year.

01:22:12.766 --> 01:22:16.516
And Stossel has invented a floating platform.

01:22:19.900 --> 01:22:22.567
They can go out far into the sea

01:22:25.008 --> 01:22:27.197
to areas where they won't interfere

01:22:27.197 --> 01:22:28.209
with people's view scape,

01:22:28.209 --> 01:22:31.116
where there's a lot of land, so to speak,

01:22:31.116 --> 01:22:32.700
but it's not land is water.

01:22:32.700 --> 01:22:34.033
And I think that

01:22:35.659 --> 01:22:38.810
that is going to come in at a price

01:22:38.810 --> 01:22:43.260
that is also less expensive than coal gas and oil

01:22:43.260 --> 01:22:44.688
and wind already is by the way,

01:22:44.688 --> 01:22:47.355
the new wind farm installations,

01:22:49.117 --> 01:22:51.117
solar is also lower cost

01:22:52.228 --> 01:22:55.450
than fossil fuel electrical generation.

01:22:55.450 --> 01:22:58.269
And I just think it's a matter of,

01:22:58.269 --> 01:22:59.908
I don't know, maybe two, three years,

01:22:59.908 --> 01:23:01.720
cause they're testing them right now.

01:23:01.720 --> 01:23:02.576
They're floating them.

01:23:02.576 --> 01:23:05.519
And Stossel is such an amazing engineer.

01:23:05.519 --> 01:23:08.939
I mean, his track record is extraordinary

01:23:08.939 --> 01:23:12.689
and when he does something, it tends to work.

01:23:14.109 --> 01:23:16.699
And somebody said, well,

01:23:16.699 --> 01:23:19.927
when he started this project over the flooding wind farms,

01:23:19.927 --> 01:23:22.040
Studies said, what if we can't afford it?

01:23:22.040 --> 01:23:23.478
And his response is

01:23:23.478 --> 01:23:25.395
we can't afford not to.

01:23:26.349 --> 01:23:28.199
So I think there is that

01:23:28.199 --> 01:23:31.749
again that inflection point where we're going right now,

01:23:31.749 --> 01:23:33.960
which is a week quibbling

01:23:33.960 --> 01:23:36.749
over a half a cent per kilowatt hour,

01:23:36.749 --> 01:23:38.088
that the coal is cheaper

01:23:38.088 --> 01:23:39.469
than it's not,

01:23:39.469 --> 01:23:41.508
but I mean are we gonna like,

01:23:41.508 --> 01:23:43.190
get, I mean, it's cheap,

01:23:43.190 --> 01:23:45.658
let's buy it. Let's do it, end of subject.

01:23:45.658 --> 01:23:46.706
Yeah.

01:23:46.706 --> 01:23:49.361
It just seems like there should be massive,

01:23:49.361 --> 01:23:53.320
wind farms offshore and then like sort of,

01:23:53.320 --> 01:23:55.520
areas of the planet

01:23:55.520 --> 01:23:56.629
where there's tons of wind

01:23:56.629 --> 01:23:58.817
and low population density

01:23:58.817 --> 01:24:02.919
and throughout the desert solar farms everywhere.

01:24:02.919 --> 01:24:03.752
There will be.

01:24:03.752 --> 01:24:04.585
I think there will be.

01:24:04.585 --> 01:24:06.835
The interesting thing about

01:24:08.496 --> 01:24:11.231
we think of things like death spiral,

01:24:11.231 --> 01:24:15.931
something, there's also other types of spirals,

01:24:15.931 --> 01:24:18.264
which is disruption spirals.

01:24:19.181 --> 01:24:22.880
And we're in a disruption spiral with wind and solar,

01:24:22.880 --> 01:24:24.223
renewable energy.

01:24:24.223 --> 01:24:26.480
Which is it's cost is going down.

01:24:26.480 --> 01:24:29.730
As this cost goes down, demand goes up.

01:24:31.641 --> 01:24:35.891
As demand goes up, demand for fossil fuel goes down

01:24:36.818 --> 01:24:40.151
as that declines, the cost goes up okay?

01:24:41.513 --> 01:24:43.722
For fossil fuels.

01:24:43.722 --> 01:24:44.700
It doesn't go down.

01:24:44.700 --> 01:24:46.331
It goes up if you use less of something

01:24:46.331 --> 01:24:47.742
than you're producing.

01:24:47.742 --> 01:24:50.159
And so as the demand goes up,

01:24:52.981 --> 01:24:55.082
it attracts more investment.

01:24:55.082 --> 01:24:56.580
More capital wants to go

01:24:56.580 --> 01:24:59.060
where something is growing of course.

01:24:59.060 --> 01:25:01.822
So the cost of capital goes down.

01:25:01.822 --> 01:25:03.338
investment in the fossil fuels,

01:25:03.338 --> 01:25:06.271
which is peaking and declining.

01:25:06.271 --> 01:25:08.559
The cost of capital is going up.

01:25:08.559 --> 01:25:12.059
So these are, this is a disruption spiral.

01:25:13.362 --> 01:25:16.953
This is going to accelerate the rate of acceleration

01:25:16.953 --> 01:25:18.952
that we're seeing in renewables.

01:25:18.952 --> 01:25:22.380
And most of the projections about solar and wind

01:25:22.380 --> 01:25:26.232
or renewable have been linear, the IEA,

01:25:26.232 --> 01:25:28.682
the International Energy Agency,

01:25:28.682 --> 01:25:31.953
the world bank, similarly McKinsey as well.

01:25:31.953 --> 01:25:35.233
And last year they admitted that they had been wrong

01:25:35.233 --> 01:25:37.610
every single year for 19 years.

01:25:37.610 --> 01:25:38.581
By undershooting it?

01:25:38.581 --> 01:25:40.051
Yeah, absolutely.

01:25:40.051 --> 01:25:41.011
Every single year.

01:25:41.011 --> 01:25:42.680
Absolutely you could count on them,

01:25:42.680 --> 01:25:43.513
to get it wrong.

01:25:43.513 --> 01:25:45.272
Every one of those institutions.

01:25:45.272 --> 01:25:47.130
And they did on batteries as well

01:25:47.130 --> 01:25:49.249
storage for EBS and for...

01:25:49.249 --> 01:25:52.912
and so I think we're on a threshold

01:25:52.912 --> 01:25:56.061
where we're going to be surprised

01:25:56.061 --> 01:25:59.394
at the rate of growth in solar and wind.

01:26:00.359 --> 01:26:04.879
And the only thing that is going to not prevent it

01:26:04.879 --> 01:26:08.713
because it's simply a capital formation

01:26:08.713 --> 01:26:10.260
and capital wants to go to it

01:26:10.260 --> 01:26:12.422
because it really pencils out.

01:26:12.422 --> 01:26:14.529
And the demand is there.

01:26:14.529 --> 01:26:17.779
A big piece with energy on this quest

01:26:18.970 --> 01:26:20.639
to electrify everything,

01:26:20.639 --> 01:26:23.661
which is a big point that you make in the book

01:26:23.661 --> 01:26:25.582
is storage, right?

01:26:25.582 --> 01:26:26.691
So we have these batteries,

01:26:26.691 --> 01:26:28.011
batteries are improving.

01:26:28.011 --> 01:26:31.799
They're growing in their capacity to hold storage,

01:26:31.799 --> 01:26:33.622
but they also degenerate

01:26:33.622 --> 01:26:36.960
and they require tons of mineral inputs,

01:26:36.960 --> 01:26:39.678
which of course have downstream impacts on the planet.

01:26:39.678 --> 01:26:43.549
Like there is no perfect panacea situation here

01:26:43.549 --> 01:26:46.077
if we improve our batteries

01:26:46.077 --> 01:26:48.561
and then everybody's got batteries for everything,

01:26:48.561 --> 01:26:51.960
we're pivoting away from drilling and fracking

01:26:51.960 --> 01:26:53.210
to just mining,

01:26:54.530 --> 01:26:55.980
which is what we're already seeing.

01:26:55.980 --> 01:26:57.713
And we're seeing a disproportionate amount of that

01:26:57.713 --> 01:27:00.310
we're seeing like China really capitalize on that

01:27:00.310 --> 01:27:01.312
in a big way right now

01:27:01.312 --> 01:27:03.596
that's creating kind of a geopolitical imbalance

01:27:03.596 --> 01:27:07.596
in terms of that certain economy is functioning.

01:27:08.449 --> 01:27:10.231
Yeah. There are things like neodymium

01:27:10.231 --> 01:27:12.564
that are integral to magnets

01:27:14.161 --> 01:27:15.473
and the magnets are integral

01:27:15.473 --> 01:27:17.810
to power generation and wind turbines.

01:27:17.810 --> 01:27:21.477
And the rare earth monopoly is almost China,

01:27:23.820 --> 01:27:25.789
not quiet really, but they really,

01:27:25.789 --> 01:27:29.272
56 or 58% of Lithium has come from China.

01:27:29.272 --> 01:27:32.939
And so they definitely have control on that.

01:27:34.380 --> 01:27:36.297
What I'm seeing is that

01:27:37.291 --> 01:27:38.731
in the like storage area

01:27:38.731 --> 01:27:40.717
where it's primarily lithium ion,

01:27:40.717 --> 01:27:42.300
and there are some,

01:27:43.209 --> 01:27:45.792
I guess what I'm saying is that

01:27:49.438 --> 01:27:52.212
there's a whole bunch of innovators

01:27:52.212 --> 01:27:55.441
circling around that one solid state batteries,

01:27:55.441 --> 01:27:57.462
carbon-based batteries no lithium

01:27:57.462 --> 01:28:00.045
and startups that are brilliant

01:28:02.338 --> 01:28:05.005
and are pointing to material use

01:28:05.851 --> 01:28:09.184
and storage capacity and cost reductions

01:28:10.661 --> 01:28:14.494
that we've seen in every single area of energy

01:28:15.589 --> 01:28:17.612
on the renewable side.

01:28:17.612 --> 01:28:21.989
Nobody thought that solar or wind would be this cheap

01:28:21.989 --> 01:28:25.840
ever not in your wildest imagination.

01:28:25.840 --> 01:28:27.304
Did anybody think that,

01:28:27.304 --> 01:28:28.811
you'd be installing solar

01:28:28.811 --> 01:28:31.504
for less than 2 cents, a kilowatt hour

01:28:31.504 --> 01:28:34.337
in the Saudi Arabia of all places.

01:28:36.281 --> 01:28:39.381
I mean, but they're doing it at a profit.

01:28:39.381 --> 01:28:41.320
I mean, it's not like subsidized.

01:28:41.320 --> 01:28:43.871
And so the same thing holds true for storage,

01:28:43.871 --> 01:28:46.109
which is that I don't think we're locked in

01:28:46.109 --> 01:28:49.680
to the technologies that we're using right now.

01:28:49.680 --> 01:28:53.142
And I think you're going to see on this storage side,

01:28:53.142 --> 01:28:55.989
I think you're gonna see techniques,

01:28:55.989 --> 01:28:57.748
technologies basically

01:28:57.748 --> 01:29:00.589
that are still gonna use minerals

01:29:00.589 --> 01:29:02.929
and minerals that are ubiquitous and easy to get

01:29:02.929 --> 01:29:06.762
as opposed to Bolivia or in the Atacama desert

01:29:08.624 --> 01:29:10.252
or things that are more sparse

01:29:10.252 --> 01:29:12.591
or not so well distributed.

01:29:12.591 --> 01:29:13.613
That's encouraging.

01:29:13.613 --> 01:29:14.839
Yeah.

01:29:14.839 --> 01:29:17.509
I feel like the progress that's being made

01:29:17.509 --> 01:29:19.459
with batteries is similar

01:29:19.459 --> 01:29:21.871
to the history of the microchip, right?

01:29:21.871 --> 01:29:23.510
It's accelerating very quickly.

01:29:23.510 --> 01:29:26.242
And every year, EVs are coming out

01:29:26.242 --> 01:29:27.651
and the battery life's getting better

01:29:27.651 --> 01:29:29.310
and better and better and et cetera,

01:29:29.310 --> 01:29:32.742
like it does feel like that's getting figured out.

01:29:32.742 --> 01:29:34.190
Yeah, it is.

01:29:34.190 --> 01:29:37.190
And we're in a huge disruptive cycle

01:29:39.311 --> 01:29:40.741
in a good sense.

01:29:40.741 --> 01:29:42.889
I mean, we're the disruptive cycle

01:29:42.889 --> 01:29:45.421
that we're this causing it is climate,

01:29:45.421 --> 01:29:49.504
but there is technologically a rate of disruption

01:29:50.990 --> 01:29:54.740
that's occurring that is hold on to your hat.

01:29:55.629 --> 01:29:56.462
The rate of change,

01:29:56.462 --> 01:29:59.795
the players, the university's engineers,

01:30:02.183 --> 01:30:03.823
the technologists, the entrepreneurs,

01:30:03.823 --> 01:30:07.460
the capital formation is extraordinary.

01:30:07.460 --> 01:30:09.377
And so nothing that we,

01:30:12.269 --> 01:30:13.909
I think are projecting in the future

01:30:13.909 --> 01:30:17.102
is going to live up to what will actually happen

01:30:17.102 --> 01:30:19.711
is gonna be more, it's gonna be better.

01:30:19.711 --> 01:30:21.621
That doesn't mean by the way, though,

01:30:21.621 --> 01:30:24.550
that we should all have an electric car,

01:30:24.550 --> 01:30:26.120
I mean, we should live in cities,

01:30:26.120 --> 01:30:27.300
we don't need a car at all, right?

01:30:27.300 --> 01:30:28.388
I mean...

01:30:28.388 --> 01:30:29.531
The 15 minute city.

01:30:29.531 --> 01:30:32.628
The 15 minute city, We should,

01:30:32.628 --> 01:30:33.611
this isn't...

01:30:33.611 --> 01:30:36.944
I feel like sometimes when you listen to

01:30:39.402 --> 01:30:40.882
some companies or some people,

01:30:40.882 --> 01:30:42.461
so some politicians

01:30:42.461 --> 01:30:44.789
that the idea is to fix it

01:30:44.789 --> 01:30:49.372
so that we can just continue to grow our economy bigger

01:30:50.222 --> 01:30:51.360
and bigger and bigger and bigger.

01:30:51.360 --> 01:30:53.572
And we just have to get rid of

01:30:53.572 --> 01:30:57.000
this nasty little problem called climate.

01:30:57.000 --> 01:30:58.869
And actually...

01:30:58.869 --> 01:30:59.702
That's kidding in a way.

01:30:59.702 --> 01:31:01.827
That's just sort of annoyingly getting in the way

01:31:01.827 --> 01:31:02.703
of economic growth.

01:31:02.703 --> 01:31:04.230
We've got to get all right,

01:31:04.230 --> 01:31:05.562
get all...

01:31:05.562 --> 01:31:06.973
but the alignment isn't with nature,

01:31:06.973 --> 01:31:08.584
the alignment is with GDP.

01:31:08.584 --> 01:31:09.417
How can we get this?

01:31:09.417 --> 01:31:12.910
And that's a very much a JP Morgan,

01:31:12.910 --> 01:31:16.160
that's very much an investment banking way

01:31:16.160 --> 01:31:17.131
of seeing the world,

01:31:17.131 --> 01:31:18.470
which is okay.

01:31:18.470 --> 01:31:19.781
It is, how can we make money doing this

01:31:19.781 --> 01:31:21.201
and doing this and doing that.

01:31:21.201 --> 01:31:22.669
But what's underlying that

01:31:22.669 --> 01:31:26.141
is like the business as usual scenarios

01:31:26.141 --> 01:31:28.141
that are used by the IAA

01:31:29.023 --> 01:31:30.950
and the world bank and the APCC

01:31:30.950 --> 01:31:33.428
basically project an economy

01:31:33.428 --> 01:31:35.902
that's two and a half times bigger by 2050

01:31:35.902 --> 01:31:38.511
and seven times bigger by 2100.

01:31:38.511 --> 01:31:40.844
And that is just ridiculous.

01:31:42.131 --> 01:31:46.881
It's so absurd to think that we can 2X, 7X, 5X, whatever,

01:31:47.761 --> 01:31:49.082
where we are today

01:31:49.082 --> 01:31:51.303
in terms of throughput and economy.

01:31:51.303 --> 01:31:54.970
I mean, and so really part of the innovation

01:31:55.840 --> 01:31:57.981
has to be, how can we do more with less?

01:31:57.981 --> 01:32:01.270
How can we fulfill our needs as human beings?

01:32:01.270 --> 01:32:02.631
And we have needs,

01:32:02.631 --> 01:32:06.390
and in a way that actually lowers our footprint,

01:32:06.390 --> 01:32:07.760
not just our carbon footprint,

01:32:07.760 --> 01:32:09.411
but our whole footprint.

01:32:09.411 --> 01:32:11.411
And so, I don't see that

01:32:15.318 --> 01:32:18.870
really coming into the conversation yet

01:32:18.870 --> 01:32:21.012
in terms of regeneration.

01:32:21.012 --> 01:32:21.845
Right.

01:32:21.845 --> 01:32:25.691
How long can the economy continue to multiply at that level?

01:32:25.691 --> 01:32:28.020
Like, is this going to go on ad infinitum?

01:32:28.020 --> 01:32:30.120
Is there any conversation about...?

01:32:30.120 --> 01:32:33.203
That's the ultimate unsustainability?

01:32:36.149 --> 01:32:36.982
Absolutely.

01:32:36.982 --> 01:32:39.482
And every price of every share

01:32:42.703 --> 01:32:45.280
with some exceptions on stock exchanges

01:32:45.280 --> 01:32:49.013
is based on assumed and projected rate of growth.

01:32:49.013 --> 01:32:52.400
And there are stocks that just pay dividends,

01:32:52.400 --> 01:32:54.789
like pipeline, just pay out.

01:32:54.789 --> 01:32:56.368
So there's a few,

01:32:56.368 --> 01:32:58.623
but 99% are based on growth

01:32:58.623 --> 01:33:00.540
and imagined projected,

01:33:02.709 --> 01:33:07.688
based on the past, based on, pipe dreams, whatever.

01:33:07.688 --> 01:33:10.990
It is weird that success is predicated

01:33:10.990 --> 01:33:12.120
this idea of growth.

01:33:12.120 --> 01:33:13.983
Like why is it not okay

01:33:13.983 --> 01:33:17.231
to just have a business that does fine

01:33:17.231 --> 01:33:19.261
and pays its employees

01:33:19.261 --> 01:33:22.558
and enriches its shareholders without growing?

01:33:22.558 --> 01:33:24.550
Because you can't amass capital.

01:33:24.550 --> 01:33:25.709
Right?

