01:37:22.378 --> 01:37:24.507
And the grocery stores aren't going to buy it or whatever.

01:37:24.507 --> 01:37:26.367
Ugly food, this, that all sorts of things.

01:37:26.367 --> 01:37:29.305
But it depends, that's in Africa.

01:37:29.305 --> 01:37:31.805
But if you go to north America

01:37:33.475 --> 01:37:36.947
and the food is actually in the waste and supply chain

01:37:36.947 --> 01:37:39.478
at the consumer level.

01:37:39.478 --> 01:37:41.347
So it depends on which country,

01:37:41.347 --> 01:37:43.848
I mean, that's an aggregate number,

01:37:43.848 --> 01:37:45.637
but actually it's kind of deceptive

01:37:45.637 --> 01:37:48.838
because it doesn't apply to Europe

01:37:48.838 --> 01:37:50.585
the same way applies to Africa

01:37:50.585 --> 01:37:52.138
where you don't have coal chains.

01:37:52.138 --> 01:37:53.369
So, they don't have a coal chain

01:37:53.369 --> 01:37:54.687
that makes it hard to preserve.

01:37:54.687 --> 01:37:56.424
Sure, but in north America,

01:37:56.424 --> 01:37:58.858
in the transport, in the chain,

01:37:58.858 --> 01:38:00.718
from farm to grocery store,

01:38:00.718 --> 01:38:02.265
or what have you, somewhere along the line,

01:38:02.265 --> 01:38:03.699
stuff spoils

01:38:03.699 --> 01:38:04.819
Oh yeah 25%

01:38:04.819 --> 01:38:06.704
And then households throw away food

01:38:06.704 --> 01:38:07.847
restaurants toss out food.

01:38:07.847 --> 01:38:10.956
Yeah, they toss too much that they didn't cook.

01:38:10.956 --> 01:38:13.856
And then if you don't have completed

01:38:13.856 --> 01:38:14.987
finish your plate,

01:38:14.987 --> 01:38:15.866
they have to throw it away.

01:38:15.866 --> 01:38:18.287
Yeah, we did a podcast

01:38:18.287 --> 01:38:21.114
with this chef Daniel Hume in New York,

01:38:21.114 --> 01:38:25.764
and he's partnered with this organization called Rethink

01:38:25.764 --> 01:38:27.804
are you familiar with what they're doing?

01:38:27.804 --> 01:38:28.637
It's super interesting.

01:38:28.637 --> 01:38:30.402
I mean, I think the most interesting part

01:38:30.402 --> 01:38:33.133
of Rethink is the technological piece,

01:38:33.133 --> 01:38:35.243
because so much of waste,

01:38:35.243 --> 01:38:38.312
at least in the restaurant ecosystem

01:38:38.312 --> 01:38:42.105
is a function of lack of kind of communication

01:38:42.105 --> 01:38:44.316
and efficiencies that would allow,

01:38:44.316 --> 01:38:47.074
that waste to go towards something good.

01:38:47.074 --> 01:38:50.464
So if you can create like a network system

01:38:50.464 --> 01:38:53.154
where and sort of efficiencies

01:38:53.154 --> 01:38:55.735
for these restaurants to set aside

01:38:55.735 --> 01:38:57.195
whatever food is getting wasted

01:38:57.195 --> 01:38:59.894
so that it could be efficiently repurposed,

01:38:59.894 --> 01:39:02.170
it's an easily solvable problem

01:39:02.170 --> 01:39:06.753
with just a little bit of distribution effort involved.

01:39:08.282 --> 01:39:09.115
It's the same thing.

01:39:09.115 --> 01:39:10.613
It's reconnecting the broken strands.

01:39:10.613 --> 01:39:12.044
Reconnect the system.

01:39:12.044 --> 01:39:14.383
The way you heal a system

01:39:14.383 --> 01:39:16.514
is to connect more of it to itself.

01:39:16.514 --> 01:39:18.953
Whether it's your immune system, an ecosystem,

01:39:18.953 --> 01:39:21.599
an economic system, a social system,

01:39:21.599 --> 01:39:23.236
you connect more of it to itself

01:39:23.236 --> 01:39:24.803
and we're radically disconnected.

01:39:24.803 --> 01:39:28.495
And so when Daniel is doing there and Rethink is doing,

01:39:28.495 --> 01:39:31.444
is saying, wait a minute, here's a source,

01:39:31.444 --> 01:39:32.602
And here's the sync,

01:39:32.602 --> 01:39:34.782
here's a need and here's a use.

01:39:34.782 --> 01:39:35.795
Right.

01:39:35.795 --> 01:39:37.850
And like, what if we do this?

01:39:37.850 --> 01:39:38.683
What if we do this?

01:39:38.683 --> 01:39:39.516
What are the logistics?

01:39:39.516 --> 01:39:40.349
What's required?

01:39:40.349 --> 01:39:42.161
How does the staff have to be trained ?

01:39:42.161 --> 01:39:44.797
Once you make that shift,

01:39:44.797 --> 01:39:45.719
It works automatically.

01:39:45.719 --> 01:39:48.432
Right, you use like a human body analogy?

01:39:48.432 --> 01:39:49.959
It would be repairing neurons

01:39:49.959 --> 01:39:53.790
or basically stitching up arteries and veins.

01:39:53.790 --> 01:39:55.921
So these things can flow.

01:39:55.921 --> 01:39:57.019
Yeah, absolutely.

01:39:57.019 --> 01:39:59.269
I mean, or unblocking them.

01:40:00.687 --> 01:40:01.854
Right, yeah.

01:40:05.951 --> 01:40:07.392
Well, in invasive surgery though.

01:40:07.392 --> 01:40:08.890
You mentioned indigeneity

01:40:08.890 --> 01:40:11.140
and indigeneity is actually

01:40:12.152 --> 01:40:14.683
sort of the light motif of regeneration.

01:40:14.683 --> 01:40:17.481
Actually, I mean, there's a piece on it,

01:40:17.481 --> 01:40:20.641
but it actually it goes throughout.

01:40:20.641 --> 01:40:22.474
And the reason is that

01:40:28.800 --> 01:40:31.061
I'm white, I'm not indigenous,

01:40:31.061 --> 01:40:33.732
but actually if I trace my ancestors all the way back...

01:40:33.732 --> 01:40:35.220
You're indigenous to something.

01:40:35.220 --> 01:40:36.053
Everyone is.

01:40:36.053 --> 01:40:36.991
Everyone is.

01:40:36.991 --> 01:40:41.110
But not in my family's history or experience

01:40:41.110 --> 01:40:43.420
nothing got passed down to me

01:40:43.420 --> 01:40:45.508
about where I'm living and how to live there.

01:40:45.508 --> 01:40:46.341
Right.

01:40:46.341 --> 01:40:49.810
And the thing that we don't understand,

01:40:49.810 --> 01:40:52.551
and I think we're starting to of course,

01:40:52.551 --> 01:40:54.301
from the horrific way

01:40:55.361 --> 01:40:58.272
that indigenous people have and are being treated

01:40:58.272 --> 01:40:59.272
to this day,

01:41:01.567 --> 01:41:04.734
is that they were amongst many things.

01:41:05.783 --> 01:41:09.200
And I don't want to reduce it in any way,

01:41:11.006 --> 01:41:14.367
but there's some of the best observational scientists

01:41:14.367 --> 01:41:16.047
in the world.

01:41:16.047 --> 01:41:19.547
And most of the science we rely upon today

01:41:20.465 --> 01:41:21.432
is empirical science,

01:41:21.432 --> 01:41:24.515
which is, it's not science, unless you can repeat it.

01:41:24.515 --> 01:41:25.873
If not repeatable,

01:41:25.873 --> 01:41:27.384
then it's just an observation.

01:41:27.384 --> 01:41:28.975
It's just an happenstance.

01:41:28.975 --> 01:41:30.332
It's just an...

01:41:30.332 --> 01:41:34.385
and but the fact is in nature, nothing repeats,

01:41:34.385 --> 01:41:36.604
and there is no lines and there's no circles.

01:41:36.604 --> 01:41:39.836
And there's all the things that we rely upon,

01:41:39.836 --> 01:41:41.464
but there's pattern

01:41:41.464 --> 01:41:44.131
and there's pattern recognition.

01:41:45.014 --> 01:41:49.847
And pattern recognition depends on time narrative stories,

01:41:52.245 --> 01:41:54.662
oral traditions that pass on,

01:41:55.633 --> 01:41:57.535
what is known language

01:41:57.535 --> 01:42:01.285
in which the teaching of how to live in place

01:42:02.983 --> 01:42:05.575
is embedded in the language

01:42:05.575 --> 01:42:09.992
so that you have verbs that are essentially metaphors

01:42:11.485 --> 01:42:13.836
for how to know where you are.

01:42:13.836 --> 01:42:15.926
And it was Aristotle who said,

01:42:15.926 --> 01:42:18.114
metaphor is genius.

01:42:18.114 --> 01:42:20.781
And so when you start to look at

01:42:22.423 --> 01:42:25.673
the 5,000 indigenous cultures on earth,

01:42:28.215 --> 01:42:29.826
and you start to see

01:42:29.826 --> 01:42:33.076
that they had extraordinarily knowledge

01:42:34.406 --> 01:42:36.501
of the place that they lived.

01:42:36.501 --> 01:42:38.423
How do we know that?

01:42:38.423 --> 01:42:39.265
Because they'd been there

01:42:39.265 --> 01:42:42.182
for 10, 12 thousand years or longer

01:42:43.755 --> 01:42:45.395
in some cases and so forth

01:42:45.395 --> 01:42:48.114
and through changes in weather

01:42:48.114 --> 01:42:50.447
and all sorts of adaptation.

01:42:52.015 --> 01:42:55.414
And so we have been the opposite.

01:42:55.414 --> 01:42:58.746
We've actually, haven't lived in many places for very long.

01:42:58.746 --> 01:43:00.824
But even if we have, sorry to interrupt,

01:43:00.824 --> 01:43:02.183
like even if we had,

01:43:02.183 --> 01:43:04.325
let's say, we've lived here for thousands of years.

01:43:04.325 --> 01:43:07.024
We're not connected to the biosphere

01:43:07.024 --> 01:43:11.485
in the manner in which those cultures were and are.

01:43:11.485 --> 01:43:12.344
They had to be

01:43:12.344 --> 01:43:16.213
'cause that's the only way they could survive.

01:43:16.213 --> 01:43:19.006
Is to actually know where they were.

01:43:19.006 --> 01:43:22.423
And so you see things like 3000 years ago

01:43:24.022 --> 01:43:28.047
on the east coast and Lyla June who is on our board,

01:43:28.047 --> 01:43:32.547
actually Denae and Cherokee activist, poet and scholar

01:43:33.654 --> 01:43:35.474
wrote a piece called the forest is farm.

01:43:35.474 --> 01:43:36.523
In what he talked about

01:43:36.523 --> 01:43:39.248
was that if you do core samples in Tennessee

01:43:39.248 --> 01:43:40.702
and around the east coast,

01:43:40.702 --> 01:43:42.374
you find 3000 years ago,

01:43:42.374 --> 01:43:44.541
a real shift in the pollen

01:43:45.526 --> 01:43:48.465
and what was being grown in different trees

01:43:48.465 --> 01:43:50.315
and different plants and animals

01:43:50.315 --> 01:43:52.637
and food plants, and like what happened

01:43:52.637 --> 01:43:57.005
and what happened is that native Americans,

01:43:57.005 --> 01:43:57.838
in this case,

01:43:57.838 --> 01:44:02.255
indigenous people transformed the forests into farms.

01:44:03.126 --> 01:44:04.865
It was, that's where they got all the food

01:44:04.865 --> 01:44:06.717
along with animals, all right.

01:44:06.717 --> 01:44:09.217
And I was on a plane to Alaska

01:44:12.333 --> 01:44:14.046
to give a talk at the university.

01:44:14.046 --> 01:44:16.354
And I was sitting next to a woman

01:44:16.354 --> 01:44:19.635
and we began to talk and she's Yupik.

01:44:19.635 --> 01:44:22.263
And the Yupik people live on the Bering Strait

01:44:22.263 --> 01:44:25.180
on both sides of Alaska and Russia.

01:44:26.923 --> 01:44:29.173
And she was going out there

01:44:30.882 --> 01:44:32.075
because her sister had died

01:44:32.075 --> 01:44:33.396
and she became the elder.

01:44:33.396 --> 01:44:36.594
And we started talking about it,

01:44:36.594 --> 01:44:40.524
how it is to live on the Bering Strait, come on.

01:44:40.524 --> 01:44:41.357
Right.

01:44:41.357 --> 01:44:43.676
And she said that to live there,

01:44:43.676 --> 01:44:45.531
they needed to be able to predict the weather

01:44:45.531 --> 01:44:47.281
two years in advance.

01:44:48.143 --> 01:44:49.293
What!

01:44:49.293 --> 01:44:50.924
I was like, wait a minute.

01:44:50.924 --> 01:44:52.195
I said, two years.

01:44:52.195 --> 01:44:56.723
She said, oh yes, she's very humble, quiet woman.

01:44:56.723 --> 01:45:00.583
And it wasn't, there was no bragging or pride,

01:45:00.583 --> 01:45:01.416
nothing, nothing.

01:45:01.416 --> 01:45:03.013
It was just matter of fact,

01:45:03.013 --> 01:45:05.114
I said, well, how'd you do that?

01:45:05.114 --> 01:45:07.714
And she started to name every single thing

01:45:07.714 --> 01:45:11.631
that they saw over a course of a year observed.

01:45:14.433 --> 01:45:17.773
And whether it was the color of the sea ice,

01:45:17.773 --> 01:45:19.856
when it froze in the fall

01:45:21.484 --> 01:45:24.303
and then melted in the spring,

01:45:24.303 --> 01:45:26.163
the depth of it, the extent of it,

01:45:26.163 --> 01:45:29.830
the type of velvet there was on the caribou,

01:45:30.714 --> 01:45:34.047
the fish, they could tell from the fish,

01:45:35.335 --> 01:45:38.603
how they had changed or subtle differences.

01:45:38.603 --> 01:45:40.835
I mean, she just started naming the clouds.

01:45:40.835 --> 01:45:42.083
They would look at the clouds.

01:45:42.083 --> 01:45:44.542
They will look, she just named every single thing

01:45:44.542 --> 01:45:45.854
in their environment.

01:45:45.854 --> 01:45:49.723
And over obviously hundreds and thousands of years,

01:45:49.723 --> 01:45:50.855
they began to say,

01:45:50.855 --> 01:45:52.583
well, this, you put this and this,

01:45:52.583 --> 01:45:53.601
when you've got those things,

01:45:53.601 --> 01:45:56.477
that means that in or in the future,

01:45:56.477 --> 01:45:59.995
or they had the memory of what happened two years ago.

01:45:59.995 --> 01:46:02.284
So they had this type of weather,

01:46:02.284 --> 01:46:03.875
they look back and say, yeah,

01:46:03.875 --> 01:46:05.596
all I can remember in one of my....

01:46:05.596 --> 01:46:08.083
And so these, and she said,

01:46:08.083 --> 01:46:10.034
and they did it to survive.

01:46:10.034 --> 01:46:11.355
Is that there was no,

01:46:11.355 --> 01:46:14.495
and we can't predict it six weeks in advance here,

01:46:14.495 --> 01:46:15.714
even two weeks in advance.

01:46:15.714 --> 01:46:17.593
That's so wild

01:46:17.593 --> 01:46:19.836
So it was amazing to know...

01:46:19.836 --> 01:46:20.669
And so beautiful.

01:46:20.669 --> 01:46:21.502
Yeah.

01:46:21.502 --> 01:46:24.085
To be so intricately involved

01:46:25.734 --> 01:46:27.275
and immersed in your environment

01:46:27.275 --> 01:46:30.453
and to be so present and paying such close attention

01:46:30.453 --> 01:46:31.704
to the smallest details

01:46:31.704 --> 01:46:34.546
and to extract meaning out of that.

01:46:34.546 --> 01:46:38.235
But they're just not just, they're just one culture.

01:46:38.235 --> 01:46:39.636
Right, right.

01:46:39.636 --> 01:46:40.469
It's true.

01:46:40.469 --> 01:46:44.802
And to have Western culture be dismissive of that,

01:46:45.651 --> 01:46:47.482
because it doesn't meet the criteria

01:46:47.482 --> 01:46:50.281
of the quote unquote scientific method.

01:46:50.281 --> 01:46:51.114
Right.

01:46:51.114 --> 01:46:51.947
Exactly.

01:46:51.947 --> 01:46:52.855
And now of course,

01:46:52.855 --> 01:46:55.133
a lot of scientists are actually turning

01:46:55.133 --> 01:46:57.095
to indigenous people,

01:46:57.095 --> 01:47:01.014
but a lot of indigenous knowledge has been lost.

01:47:01.014 --> 01:47:02.944
I mean, the elders died

01:47:02.944 --> 01:47:05.916
and they were, you saw what happened in Canada,

01:47:05.916 --> 01:47:07.433
I think it could happen here too.

01:47:07.433 --> 01:47:09.633
Like their children were taken away,

01:47:09.633 --> 01:47:12.923
they were murdered essentially in Catholic schools,

01:47:12.923 --> 01:47:16.795
I mean, they were traumatized, they were belittled.

01:47:16.795 --> 01:47:20.835
They were taken away from their native foods.

01:47:20.835 --> 01:47:24.745
They were fed commodity foods full of sugar, fat and starch.

01:47:24.745 --> 01:47:28.474
They got obese, they got type two diabetes,

01:47:28.474 --> 01:47:29.824
they couldn't get jobs,

01:47:29.824 --> 01:47:32.657
I mean all of what we did to them,

01:47:33.655 --> 01:47:35.563
it wasn't them doing it to themselves.

01:47:35.563 --> 01:47:38.896
And so, yet in some way, in most places,

01:47:39.994 --> 01:47:43.335
they were able to maintain and keep this knowledge.

01:47:43.335 --> 01:47:45.184
And these oral traditions passed on

01:47:45.184 --> 01:47:48.775
generation for hundreds and hundreds of years,

01:47:48.775 --> 01:47:52.196
from being harassed and hated by the colonists.

01:47:52.196 --> 01:47:55.613
And so now I think we're realizing, gosh,

01:47:58.675 --> 01:48:02.008
this is Alexandria library of knowledge.

01:48:03.005 --> 01:48:05.923
that was right there all along,

01:48:05.923 --> 01:48:07.164
except we didn't listen.

01:48:07.164 --> 01:48:10.096
No, instead we dislocated them,

01:48:10.096 --> 01:48:12.476
marginalized them, stole their land

01:48:12.476 --> 01:48:14.046
and forced them into situations

01:48:14.046 --> 01:48:16.046
where they have to open casinos.

01:48:16.046 --> 01:48:17.965
Absolutely. Yeah.

01:48:17.965 --> 01:48:21.636
And so, I mean, part of regeneration

01:48:21.636 --> 01:48:26.072
people say, well, what does that have to do with climate

01:48:26.072 --> 01:48:28.405
is absolutely to make amends

01:48:30.525 --> 01:48:32.927
in whatever way we can today,

01:48:32.927 --> 01:48:34.555
not just to indigenous people,

01:48:34.555 --> 01:48:37.156
but actually, well, not to us,

01:48:37.156 --> 01:48:38.343
I mean...

01:48:38.343 --> 01:48:39.363
To the planet itself.

01:48:39.363 --> 01:48:40.265
But African African-Americans

01:48:40.265 --> 01:48:42.215
because they're indigenous people who were enslaved

01:48:42.215 --> 01:48:43.457
and brought over.

01:48:43.457 --> 01:48:46.737
There's 3000 different cultures in Africa.

01:48:46.737 --> 01:48:49.154
And when we look at the roots

01:48:50.365 --> 01:48:52.016
of regenerative agriculture in this country,

01:48:52.016 --> 01:48:55.685
I mean the initial roots came from Africa

01:48:55.685 --> 01:48:57.076
it didn't come from Asia.

01:48:57.076 --> 01:48:59.055
They didn't come from any place else.

01:48:59.055 --> 01:49:02.805
And can you imagine being a woman in Ghana

01:49:02.805 --> 01:49:04.773
and for whatever reason

01:49:04.773 --> 01:49:08.023
you've been kidnapped and then hurtled,

01:49:10.604 --> 01:49:13.504
and then you're put on a boat

01:49:13.504 --> 01:49:16.705
going to somewhere you don't know

01:49:16.705 --> 01:49:18.185
that nobody can explain to you

01:49:18.185 --> 01:49:19.927
that makes no sense at all.

01:49:19.927 --> 01:49:23.544
And somehow these women braided rice

01:49:23.544 --> 01:49:25.627
and seeds into their hair

01:49:26.993 --> 01:49:30.806
and brought them to so-called new world.

01:49:30.806 --> 01:49:32.916
and which is why the African-Americans

01:49:32.916 --> 01:49:37.057
were the best rice farmers in all of Southeast America.

01:49:37.057 --> 01:49:38.914
And when I started Erewhon

01:49:38.914 --> 01:49:41.581
and started to grow organic rice

01:49:42.738 --> 01:49:45.273
or get it grown actually for us,

01:49:45.273 --> 01:49:48.496
I work with two black farmers in Arkansas

01:49:48.496 --> 01:49:52.217
and their knowledge of rice was unbelievable.

01:49:52.217 --> 01:49:54.356
And it went back for almost 400 years.

01:49:54.356 --> 01:49:55.404
Wow!

01:49:55.404 --> 01:49:56.258
And so...

01:49:56.258 --> 01:49:57.343
That's a wild story.

01:49:57.343 --> 01:49:58.263
I know we don't,

01:49:58.263 --> 01:50:01.137
we have no idea where our food waste came from

01:50:01.137 --> 01:50:02.626
or where the knowledge came from.

01:50:02.626 --> 01:50:05.046
Of course, George Washington, Carver got that.

01:50:05.046 --> 01:50:07.136
Right. I noticed him in the book too.

01:50:07.136 --> 01:50:08.755
Like, talk about that a little bit.

01:50:08.755 --> 01:50:11.446
Cause that's, I did not know about this either.

01:50:11.446 --> 01:50:15.029
Well, I mean, he was the one who observed

01:50:16.982 --> 01:50:20.399
that the difference between black farmers

01:50:22.154 --> 01:50:23.576
and the results they were getting

01:50:23.576 --> 01:50:27.843
and why farmers who were mostly cotton farmers,

01:50:27.843 --> 01:50:30.953
cause it was the best cotton soil

01:50:30.953 --> 01:50:32.870
where they were Alabama

01:50:34.576 --> 01:50:37.723
and how they're they're playing out their farms

01:50:37.723 --> 01:50:40.576
they were killing the soil, killing the farm.

01:50:40.576 --> 01:50:43.243
And then on a nourishment level,

01:50:44.526 --> 01:50:46.111
then of course, on the peanuts,

01:50:46.111 --> 01:50:47.284
you're looking at groundnuts

01:50:47.284 --> 01:50:50.607
as a source of both restoring soil,

01:50:50.607 --> 01:50:51.555
but also restoring,

01:50:51.555 --> 01:50:56.046
inexpensive way to provide a balanced nutrient

01:50:56.046 --> 01:50:59.567
to people that they weren't getting at that time.

01:50:59.567 --> 01:51:01.903
And, but it wasn't just him.

01:51:01.903 --> 01:51:05.054
He had a whole bunch of other colleagues,

01:51:05.054 --> 01:51:07.387
Tuskegee, they work together

01:51:08.396 --> 01:51:11.586
and again, the roots of that,

01:51:11.586 --> 01:51:13.919
and the benefit of that is,

01:51:16.450 --> 01:51:18.293
throughout America to this day,

01:51:18.293 --> 01:51:20.264
and we don't recognize.

01:51:20.264 --> 01:51:21.413
Right.

01:51:21.413 --> 01:51:22.830
I mean, when you,

01:51:24.285 --> 01:51:26.674
I like not to keep bringing it back to this documentary,

01:51:26.674 --> 01:51:29.063
but when you watch the biggest little farm

01:51:29.063 --> 01:51:32.344
short of John and Molly finding some version of that,

01:51:32.344 --> 01:51:36.511
like somebody who's steeped enough in soil ecology

01:51:39.752 --> 01:51:41.557
to help guide them.

01:51:41.557 --> 01:51:44.175
They would've never been able to make it work there.

01:51:44.175 --> 01:51:46.654
And to think that there's this robust,

01:51:46.654 --> 01:51:50.321
unbelievable cannon, a library of Alexandria

01:51:51.745 --> 01:51:54.186
of this kind of wisdom and knowledge

01:51:54.186 --> 01:51:57.436
based on millennia of observing nature,

01:51:58.755 --> 01:52:00.672
that it's lost is crazy

01:52:01.686 --> 01:52:04.846
because anybody who would carry that message

01:52:04.846 --> 01:52:07.853
would be so valuable in so many ways

01:52:07.853 --> 01:52:12.016
to anybody who's looking to tread a little bit more lightly

01:52:12.016 --> 01:52:13.614
on the planet and to do things

01:52:13.614 --> 01:52:16.694
in a regenerative symbiotic way.

01:52:16.694 --> 01:52:20.926
And again, the other thing that we've lost,

01:52:20.926 --> 01:52:23.495
maybe lost respect for is the language itself,

01:52:23.495 --> 01:52:25.207
because there wasn't a written,

01:52:25.207 --> 01:52:27.604
so it's not really an add-on or whatever.

01:52:27.604 --> 01:52:29.535
And I remember being in Patagonia

01:52:29.535 --> 01:52:33.973
and grinders museum and the Yamanote south Vietnam people,

01:52:33.973 --> 01:52:35.154
depending on which name you use,

01:52:35.154 --> 01:52:36.662
but, and there was like,

01:52:36.662 --> 01:52:40.176
I don't know, maybe a few dozen left at that time.

01:52:40.176 --> 01:52:43.525
I mean, there had been extirpated basically

01:52:43.525 --> 01:52:45.925
by disease and by guns.

01:52:45.925 --> 01:52:50.093
And Bruce Chatwin wrote a book in Patagonia

01:52:50.093 --> 01:52:54.093
about the Yamen people and about their language.

01:52:55.126 --> 01:52:56.903
And then I have a friend

01:52:56.903 --> 01:52:57.844
who was, I grew up

01:52:57.844 --> 01:52:59.363
at the university of California libraries,

01:52:59.363 --> 01:53:00.253
where my father worked

01:53:00.253 --> 01:53:04.136
in my family, marrying the librarian was hero.

01:53:04.136 --> 01:53:06.219
She was really something.

01:53:07.182 --> 01:53:08.674
And so I spent a lot of time at the library.

01:53:08.674 --> 01:53:11.404
And so I understood from Bruce's book

01:53:11.404 --> 01:53:12.822
that there was a dictionary

01:53:12.822 --> 01:53:15.034
that there was a missionary who was down there

01:53:15.034 --> 01:53:16.535
who had nothing to do.

01:53:16.535 --> 01:53:20.598
And there was only a hundred or so Yamana people left

01:53:20.598 --> 01:53:21.931
and he was a lexicographer.

01:53:21.931 --> 01:53:23.358
So he made a dictionary

01:53:23.358 --> 01:53:24.859
and worked with the tribal chief

01:53:24.859 --> 01:53:27.088
but the tribal chief was really

01:53:27.088 --> 01:53:28.461
that killed so many people.

01:53:28.461 --> 01:53:31.337
He was like by default, but he wasn't.

01:53:31.337 --> 01:53:33.170
And so for many years,

01:53:34.289 --> 01:53:36.759
he made a dictionary of the Yamana language

01:53:36.759 --> 01:53:38.599
into English Dictionary.

01:53:38.599 --> 01:53:39.748
It was really amazing dictionary.

01:53:39.748 --> 01:53:42.127
And they got to 30,000 words

01:53:42.127 --> 01:53:43.775
and the missionary died.

01:53:43.775 --> 01:53:47.346
And the Yamuna wouldn't talk to them about cosmology,

01:53:47.346 --> 01:53:49.176
about women's issues and so forth.

01:53:49.176 --> 01:53:52.576
They said, no, we don't trust you with this knowledge,

01:53:52.576 --> 01:53:53.559
but they did talk about,

01:53:53.559 --> 01:53:55.849
and if you read this dictionary,

01:53:55.849 --> 01:53:57.787
it's like I said, it's a metaphor.

01:53:57.787 --> 01:53:59.375
And like depression,

01:53:59.375 --> 01:54:01.056
the word for depression

01:54:01.056 --> 01:54:04.574
is a crab that's molting it's shell,

01:54:04.574 --> 01:54:06.988
but it hasn't come off yet.

01:54:06.988 --> 01:54:08.234
I mean, get out of here.

01:54:08.234 --> 01:54:09.067
What!

01:54:09.067 --> 01:54:12.227
I mean, it's such an exquisite language,

01:54:12.227 --> 01:54:13.346
30,000 words.

01:54:13.346 --> 01:54:16.746
And these are the people that Darwin called beasts

01:54:16.746 --> 01:54:20.055
because when they came around, Tero De Fuego,

01:54:20.055 --> 01:54:21.588
which it wasn't named then,

01:54:21.588 --> 01:54:24.128
it was like they saw fire and smoke

01:54:24.128 --> 01:54:26.528
and the foreign smoke was from the Yamana

01:54:26.528 --> 01:54:28.586
who carried fire with them everywhere.

01:54:28.586 --> 01:54:29.815
Cause it was cold,

01:54:29.815 --> 01:54:31.146
but they were naked

01:54:31.146 --> 01:54:32.568
because they put seal fat

01:54:32.568 --> 01:54:35.009
all over the body to stay warm.

01:54:35.009 --> 01:54:36.366
And they knew that clothing

01:54:36.366 --> 01:54:39.717
would actually get wet and actually caused them to die.

01:54:39.717 --> 01:54:41.856
So they were called beasts, right?

01:54:41.856 --> 01:54:45.728
And they acted in strange ways and so forth.

01:54:45.728 --> 01:54:47.895
Japanese has 40,000 words.

01:54:49.586 --> 01:54:53.759
This dictionary had 30,000 words and is incomplete.

01:54:53.759 --> 01:54:54.810
So...

01:54:54.810 --> 01:54:55.643
It's wild.

01:54:55.643 --> 01:54:59.158
So our understanding of indigeneity

01:54:59.158 --> 01:55:02.417
of indigenous people is so broken.

01:55:02.417 --> 01:55:03.935
And so...

01:55:03.935 --> 01:55:08.135
Well it's metered in condescension, essentially.

01:55:08.135 --> 01:55:09.708
They were not educated

01:55:09.708 --> 01:55:11.888
in the manner in which we recognize,

01:55:11.888 --> 01:55:14.077
so we're dismissive of them.

01:55:14.077 --> 01:55:17.486
Yeah. We don't have the right lens to understand.

01:55:17.486 --> 01:55:18.679
We don't have the right lens to see it,

01:55:18.679 --> 01:55:19.512
understand it,

01:55:19.512 --> 01:55:20.830
and frankly benefit from it.

01:55:20.830 --> 01:55:22.663
And we were basically,

01:55:27.261 --> 01:55:29.070
we saw them as beasts.

01:55:29.070 --> 01:55:31.458
We use that word, not just Darwin

01:55:31.458 --> 01:55:33.875
and as a subclass of species,

01:55:34.730 --> 01:55:37.330
a subclass of human beings.

01:55:37.330 --> 01:55:38.378
Well, yeah.

01:55:38.378 --> 01:55:43.128
I mean, when you think of somebody like Paul Stamets,

01:55:43.128 --> 01:55:44.909
who knows everything about mushrooms,

01:55:44.909 --> 01:55:47.538
and now we're seeing this resurgence of enthusiasm

01:55:47.538 --> 01:55:49.538
and interest in mycology

01:55:50.570 --> 01:55:54.697
and what we can learn from the fungal universe,

01:55:54.697 --> 01:55:56.388
creating foods from them

01:55:56.388 --> 01:55:58.611
and just appreciating how much more complex

01:55:58.611 --> 01:56:00.240
and amazing it is

01:56:00.240 --> 01:56:02.008
than we ever imagined.

01:56:02.008 --> 01:56:02.848
But in my mind,

01:56:02.848 --> 01:56:07.298
he's in furtherance of that indigenous tradition

01:56:07.298 --> 01:56:10.099
because he's so steeped in this one thing, right?

01:56:10.099 --> 01:56:12.761
So imagine thousands of Paul Stamets,

01:56:12.761 --> 01:56:15.511
who are experts in a wide variety

01:56:16.897 --> 01:56:18.789
of different types of fields

01:56:18.789 --> 01:56:21.959
that have to do with the cycles of the planet.

01:56:21.959 --> 01:56:22.792
Yes.

01:56:22.792 --> 01:56:24.539
And those people are coming

01:56:24.539 --> 01:56:25.378
and there was,

01:56:25.378 --> 01:56:28.295
there are respectful scientists now

01:56:29.727 --> 01:56:31.747
and the Uswar and the Waorani and

01:56:31.747 --> 01:56:34.219
they're in, in service,

01:56:34.219 --> 01:56:36.680
in honor of their knowledge

01:56:36.680 --> 01:56:39.388
and time then help also

01:56:39.388 --> 01:56:41.388
to keep out, the miners,

01:56:42.468 --> 01:56:44.635
the loggers, COVID as well

01:56:46.449 --> 01:56:50.459
for that matter in the Amazon, in Peru, in Ecuador.

01:56:50.459 --> 01:56:54.359
So there are some really wonderful things

01:56:54.359 --> 01:56:57.797
going on now because of the respect

01:56:57.797 --> 01:57:00.899
and understanding that was never there

01:57:00.899 --> 01:57:04.968
or was hardly there and now is really growing.

01:57:04.968 --> 01:57:08.547
And I think it also relates to a planet on fire.

01:57:08.547 --> 01:57:11.297
And so who are you gonna turn to?

01:57:12.721 --> 01:57:15.167
Obviously not Chevron, Exxon Odyssey,

01:57:15.167 --> 01:57:16.160
not the Republican party,

01:57:16.160 --> 01:57:18.749
and obviously not people who could care less

01:57:18.749 --> 01:57:21.718
and these people who are so

01:57:21.718 --> 01:57:24.246
I kind of, I don't want to be those people,

01:57:24.246 --> 01:57:26.737
people who live in a place

01:57:26.737 --> 01:57:28.699
and have cared for it so long,

01:57:28.699 --> 01:57:31.058
have something to teach us.

01:57:31.058 --> 01:57:33.938
About caring and about knowing,

01:57:33.938 --> 01:57:37.355
and that we've lost or that we never had.

01:57:38.448 --> 01:57:41.017
So in Paul Stamets, I mean,

01:57:41.017 --> 01:57:43.760
when you think of his career and think of it,

01:57:43.760 --> 01:57:45.837
I mean, he accidentally,

01:57:45.837 --> 01:57:48.437
boiled up a whole bag full of psilocybin.

01:57:48.437 --> 01:57:50.047
Somebody gave it to him,

01:57:50.047 --> 01:57:52.279
he said just take a few,

01:57:52.279 --> 01:57:53.411
he boiled the whole bag up and drank it

01:57:53.411 --> 01:57:55.718
and then went up a tree,

01:57:55.718 --> 01:57:58.497
he was, and then the thunderstorm came

01:57:58.497 --> 01:58:01.930
and he was in that tree all night, long blowing around,

01:58:01.930 --> 01:58:03.930
and it's for him to say,

01:58:07.238 --> 01:58:12.147
but to me, I mean, Paul Stamets was born that night.

01:58:12.147 --> 01:58:14.188
Right. He had a spiritual awakening.

01:58:14.188 --> 01:58:15.636
He did, he absolutely did.

01:58:15.636 --> 01:58:17.176
And his life path unfurled.

01:58:17.176 --> 01:58:20.259
And he stuttered up until that moment.

01:58:20.259 --> 01:58:22.137
And when he came down from the tree he did not,

01:58:22.137 --> 01:58:23.216
he no longer stuttered.

01:58:23.216 --> 01:58:24.049
Right.

01:58:24.049 --> 01:58:25.132
And so that

01:58:26.479 --> 01:58:30.575
he had in a very short night or maybe a long night,

01:58:30.575 --> 01:58:32.992
but you have to be out there.

01:58:34.777 --> 01:58:36.147
You have to be in nature.

01:58:36.147 --> 01:58:40.066
You have to turn off everything that's on

01:58:40.066 --> 01:58:42.811
and turn on everything that's off.

01:58:42.811 --> 01:58:45.949
to you it's not off actually, and so forth.

01:58:45.949 --> 01:58:47.359
And spend that time.

01:58:47.359 --> 01:58:50.218
I spent three months once in silence

01:58:50.218 --> 01:58:51.408
in the woods with nobody there.

01:58:51.408 --> 01:58:52.241
You did?

01:58:52.241 --> 01:58:53.074
Yeah, yeah.

01:58:53.074 --> 01:58:54.247
When did you do that?

01:58:54.247 --> 01:58:58.490
I did that about 14 years ago, 15 years ago,

01:58:58.490 --> 01:59:01.027
So you had kind of a Walden pond experience.

01:59:01.027 --> 01:59:03.669
I was just a caretaker at a refuge

01:59:03.669 --> 01:59:05.117
that was in New Mexico

01:59:05.117 --> 01:59:07.909
and it was during the winter

01:59:07.909 --> 01:59:10.179
and early spring and nobody was there.

01:59:10.179 --> 01:59:11.868
So I didn't speak to anyone.

01:59:11.868 --> 01:59:13.929
Cause there was no one to speak to.

01:59:13.929 --> 01:59:14.878
I mean, it was easy.

01:59:14.878 --> 01:59:16.909
So you weren't setting out to have that experience.

01:59:16.909 --> 01:59:20.287
It just was an occurrence by dent of...

